window.STUDY_SEARCH={"courses":[{"slug":"classical-myth","name":"Classical Myth in Art","home":"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"color-theory","name":"Color Theory","home":"/study/color-theory.html","img":"/study/color-theory/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"composition","name":"Composition","home":"/study/composition.html","img":"/study/composition/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"fauvism-and-expressionism","name":"Fauvism and Early Expressionism","home":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"impressionism","name":"Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japanese-woodblock-prints","name":"Japanese Woodblock Prints","home":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","img":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japonisme","name":"Japonisme","home":"/study/art-history/japonisme.html","img":"/study/art-history/japonisme/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"neoclassicism","name":"Neoclassicism","home":"/study/art-history/neoclassicism.html","img":"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"oil-vs-acrylic","name":"Oil vs Acrylic","home":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","img":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"pigments-and-materials","name":"Pigments and Materials","home":"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","img":"/study/pigments-and-materials/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"post-impressionism","name":"Post-Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"realism","name":"Realism","home":"/study/art-history/realism.html","img":"/study/art-history/realism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"rococo","name":"Rococo","home":"/study/art-history/rococo.html","img":"/study/art-history/rococo/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"romanticism","name":"Romanticism","home":"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","img":"/study/art-history/romanticism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"value-and-light","name":"Value and Light","home":"/study/value-and-light.html","img":"/study/value-and-light/img/","lane":"Technique"}],"items":[[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","Classical Myth in Art · Overview","What to Look For","","h1_aphrodite","The gods and heroes of Greece and Rome have been painted and carved for some 2,400 years. These works run from an Athenian wine cup of about 530 BCE to a Paris studio picture of about 1890, and each shows one way to recognize the myth, and the maker, from what is on the surface. · Sandro Botticelli · The Birth of Venus · about 1485"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/dionysus-cup.html","Classical Myth in Art","Dionysus Cup","Exekias","h9_exekias","Exekias · about 530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, about 12 in. across. Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · Exekias (Athenian potter and painter, 6th century BCE). Look at the figures: they are flat black silhouettes, with the detail scratched into them. · Ship, god, grapes and dolphins are solid black shapes against the orange-red ground. · Lines inside the shapes, like the grape bunches and the ship's planking, are cut through the black to the clay below. · The sail is the one pale shape, so it reads as a hole of light in the upper half of the bowl. · The design has no ground line: the dolphins circle the ship at every angle, so the whole inside of the cup is sea. · The god is Dionysus; the dolphins are the pirates he turned into fish. Only the inside is shown here."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/krater-with-heracles-wrestling-antaeus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","Euphronios","h12_antaios","Euphronios · about 515–510 BCE · Red-figure terracotta, 18 in. high. Louvre, Paris · Euphronios (Athenian painter, 6th–5th century BCE). Look at the two wrestlers: they are drawn with a brush, in thin lines on the reddish clay, and the black is the background. · Muscles, ribs and knees are laid out as fine dark lines on the clay, drawn rather than scratched. · The two bodies cross as diagonals, Heracles pushing from the left, Antaeus flattened under him. · Antaeus's hair is a rough fringe and Heracles's is a cap of tiny raised dots, so the two heads read differently at a glance. · The women at either side rise upright in long dress folds, arms out, and frame the struggle between them. · Heracles kneels and strangles Antaeus; three women flee. Heracles's club, quiver and lion skin are drawn at the left."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bronze-god-from-cape-artemision.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bronze God from Cape Artemision","Greek, sculptor unknown","h1_poseidon","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 460 BCE · Bronze, 82 1/4 in. high. National Archaeological Museum, Athens · Look at the pose: the whole body is one wide stance, arms stretched out to balance a throw that is about to happen. · The arms reach almost the figure's full height side to side, so the statue is as wide as it is tall. · Both feet are planted wide, one leg bent and one straight, a stride caught mid-step. · Chest and stomach are modelled as broad, firm planes, with no strain in the face. · The beard is a mass of tight curls and the hair a flat braid, the only fine detail on a plain body. · Nothing is in either hand, so it is read as Zeus with a thunderbolt or Poseidon with a trident. It was pulled from the sea off Euboea."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/apollo-belvedere.html","Classical Myth in Art","Apollo Belvedere","Roman, after a Greek bronze","h1_apollo","Roman, after a Greek bronze · early 2nd century CE · Marble. Vatican Museums, Vatican City · Look at the line of the body: it bends in a slow S from the turned head down through the hip to the back foot. · The head turns one way while the outstretched arm reaches the other, so the figure twists instead of facing you square. · One leg carries the weight and the other trails, which tips the hips and starts the curve. · The cloak hangs from the shoulder in deep, shadowed folds that set off the smooth skin beside them. · The surface is worked to an even white, and the face stays calm; nothing in the carving suggests effort. · A Roman copy of a Greek bronze of about 330 BCE. Apollo has just loosed an arrow; the outstretched hand once held the bow."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/venus-de-milo.html","Classical Myth in Art","Venus de Milo","Greek, sculptor unknown","h6_milo","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 150–125 BCE · Parian marble, 80 1/4 in. high. Louvre, Paris · Look at the twist: the hips turn one way and the shoulders the other, so the figure is a spiral you have to walk around. · The weight is on one hip, so the stomach curves and one side of the waist folds in. · The torso is carved as rounded, weighty masses, with soft transitions and no hard edges. · The cloth bunches in thick ridges at the hips, a heavy block that holds the smooth body above it up. · The skin surface is slightly grainy, so it catches light more gently than a polish would. · Carved in two blocks joined at the hips. Both arms are missing, all but a stub."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-birth-of-venus.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Birth of Venus","Sandro Botticelli","h1_aphrodite","Sandro Botticelli · about 1485 · Tempera on canvas, 68 × 109 3/4 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Sandro Botticelli (Italian, 1445–1510). Look at the outlines: every figure is drawn with a clean contour, and the colour inside is nearly flat. · A dark line runs round the goddess's body, hair and shell, so she reads as a cut-out shape on the sea. · The sea is a sheet of pale green with small white V-marks for waves, more pattern than water. · The figures hardly cast shadows, and the light comes from nowhere in particular. · Hair, cloaks and falling roses are pulled into long curling lines that all show the same wind, blowing in from the left. · Venus comes ashore on a shell, blown by the wind gods; the woman at right holds out her cloak."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/diana-and-actaeon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Diana and Actaeon","Titian","h6_actaeon","Titian · 1556–59 · Oil on canvas, 72 3/4 × 79 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland · Titian (Italian, about 1488–1576). Look at how the picture is split: the hunter stands alone at the left and the goddess's group fills the right. · A pulled-back red curtain and a stone pillar divide the canvas into two unequal halves. · The hunter's raised hand is higher than any head in the picture; the bathers' heads sit lower, bunched across the right. · The pale bodies are packed together in the lower right, so the eye lands there after the hunter. · The blue sky shows mainly through the arch, a bright patch that fills the gap between the two sides. · One of six myth paintings for Philip II of Spain, known as the poesie, with subjects from Ovid."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-rape-of-europa.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Rape of Europa","Titian","h5_europa","Titian · 1559–62 · Oil on canvas, 70 × 80 3/4 in. Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · Look at the brushwork: the red scarf, the clouds and the water are dragged on in loose strokes that never tidy up into detail. · The scarf at upper right is a few wide sweeps of pink and white that stay visible as strokes. · The distant mountains and shore are smudged into the sky with almost no edge between them. · The bull's head, the woman and the cupids are brought to a finish; sky, water and shore are left open. · Her pose is a loose diagonal across the lower right, legs thrown one way and arm the other. · Also painted for Philip II; signed at lower left, under the cupid on the dolphin."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bacchus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bacchus","Caravaggio","h9_bacchus","Caravaggio · about 1598 · Oil on canvas, 37 1/2 × 33 1/2 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Caravaggio (Italian, 1571–1610). Look at the light: one source from the upper left falls on skin, cloth and fruit, and the wall behind drops away into brown. · The bare shoulder at left is the brightest patch in the picture, and the face is turned so its shaded side falls away from it. · The white drapery is modelled in big folds with hard lights and deep creases, like carved marble. · The fruit bowl is painted as closely as the face, with bruises and wormholes left in. · The wine glass is held out toward you, at the front edge of the picture. · A youth crowned with vine leaves as the wine god, offering a glass."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-fall-of-phaeton.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Fall of Phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens","h3_phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens · about 1604–05, probably reworked about 1606–08 · Oil on canvas, 38 3/4 × 51 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Peter Paul Rubens (Flemish, 1577–1640). Look at the composition: everything tumbles along one diagonal, from the rearing horse at upper right down to the tangle of horses at lower left. · Horses, chariot and bodies are bunched into a single swirling mass with no still point. · Shafts of light cut across the scene from the upper right, slicing through the clouds. · Figures at the edges are cropped by the frame, as if the action spills out of the picture. · The paint is thin and sketchy in the clouds and thicker on the horses, so the centre has the most weight. · The sun's chariot breaks apart; the butterfly-winged women at left are the seasons, in terror."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/laocoon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Laocoön","El Greco","h15_laocoon","El Greco · about 1610–14 · Oil on canvas, 54 × 68 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · El Greco (Greek, 1541–1614). Look at the shapes of the bodies: they are stretched long and thin, and their edges flicker. · Legs and torsos are pulled out past natural length, so the figures look taller than they could stand. · The flesh is grey-white with streaks of darker grey, lit from no clear direction. · The sky is painted in broken, sweeping strokes of blue, grey and white, as restless as the figures. · The city sits small in the middle distance, in hard little shapes, while the figures loom huge in front. · The Trojan priest and his sons are attacked by snakes; the city behind is Toledo."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/vulcans-forge.html","Classical Myth in Art","Vulcan's Forge","Diego Velázquez","h1_hephaestus","Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Oil on canvas, 87 3/4 × 114 1/4 in. Museo del Prado, Madrid · Diego Velázquez (Spanish, 1599–1660). Look at the paint handling: the god at left is a glow of thin, quick strokes, while the smiths are built up solidly. · Rays around Apollo's head are flicked on in short pale lines over the wall. · His orange robe is painted in long fluid strokes, with the folds barely described. · The smiths' backs and arms are modelled firmly in warm light and cool shadow, every muscle in place. · The space is made by the figures: each stands at a different depth, and the room recedes behind them. · Apollo tells the smith god that his wife is unfaithful; the workers freeze at the news."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-abduction-of-proserpina.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Abduction of Proserpina","Rembrandt","h9_bernini","Rembrandt · about 1630–31 · Oil on oak panel, 33 1/4 × 31 1/4 in. Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · Rembrandt (Dutch, 1606–1669). Look at the surface: the lit parts are thick, textured paint, and the rest sinks into a dark ground. · The woman's dress and the chariot are built up in crusted, pale strokes. · The whole right half is near-black, with only a horse's rump and some foliage picked out. · The sky at upper left is a block of pure blue against which the dark trees are silhouetted. · The light runs from the blue corner down to the horses, so the eye travels out of the day and into the dark. · Pluto drags the girl down to the underworld; her companions cling to her cloak."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-judgement-of-paris.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Judgement of Paris","Peter Paul Rubens","h15_paris","Peter Paul Rubens · probably 1632–35 · Oil on panel, probably oak, 57 × 76 1/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Look at the colour: three warm pink bodies stand against cool greens and greys, with strong red held to the cloaks at the edges and the robe of the nude seen from behind. · Each nude is a slightly different pink, from cool and pearly at left to golden in the centre. · The evening sky behind them grades from yellow at the horizon to grey-blue above. · Silk, velvet, feathers and hair are painted with soft edges that blur into the flesh beside them. · The line of figures runs across the picture like a frieze, with the landscape opening on the right. · Paris hands the apple to Venus, in the centre; the owl marks Minerva at left and the peacock Juno at right."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-explains-the-riddle-of-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","h14_ingres","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808 · Oil on canvas, 74 1/2 × 56 3/4 in. Louvre, Paris · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres (French, 1780–1867). Look at the edge of the body: a single unbroken contour, drawn as if with a pen, with no visible brushwork inside it. · The figure is in strict profile, so the outline from brow to heel reads as one continuous line. · Skin is smoothed to an even, waxy finish. · The lit body stands against a dark cave, which makes the contour sharper still. · Colour is kept to a few flat notes: tan skin, red cloth, ochre rock, blue sky. · Oedipus answers the Sphinx's riddle; a foot and bones lie at lower left."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/ulysses-deriding-polyphemus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","J. M. W. Turner","h16_turner","J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 52 1/4 × 80 in. The National Gallery, London · J. M. W. Turner (British, 1775–1851). Look at the colour as light: the story is told by a sunrise of yellow, pink and blue, and the figures are hard to find. · The sun at right is the brightest point, and everything near it dissolves into pale yellow. · The giant is a shadowy brown-purple shape in the clouds at upper left, barely more solid than they are. · Thin, dry colour is dragged over the sky, so pinks and yellows sit on top of each other in layers. · The ship is the sharpest place in the picture: a gilded hull and crisp pale sails against the glare. · Ulysses, in red under a red banner, taunts the giant; sea nymphs glimmer at the bow."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-and-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus and the Sphinx","Gustave Moreau","h18_moreau","Gustave Moreau · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 81 1/4 × 41 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gustave Moreau (French, 1826–1898). Look at the surface detail: the wings, the jewels and the urn are picked out feather by feather and bead by bead. · Every edge is sharp, from the wing tips to the red cord of beads around the creature's chest. · The strokes are tiny and build up ornament rather than texture. · The two heads are locked eye to eye at the top of a tall, narrow canvas, which squeezes the drama into a vertical. · The background rocks are cool grey-blue, so the gold-brown body and the pale flesh come forward. · The same riddle scene as two slides back, now a close standoff; shown at the Paris Salon of 1864."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/pygmalion-and-galatea.html","Classical Myth in Art","Pygmalion and Galatea","Jean-Léon Gérôme","h6_pygmalion","Jean-Léon Gérôme · about 1890 · Oil on canvas, 35 × 27 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Jean-Léon Gérôme (French, 1824–1904). Look at the colour of the statue: it is chalk white at the feet and warms to living flesh as it rises. · The change from marble to skin happens in a smooth gradient up the legs, with no line where one stops. · The sculptor's blue tunic is the strongest colour, set against the grey-green upper wall. · The surface is as smooth as the statue it shows, even across the whole picture. · The pair are seen from behind, so the kiss is half hidden and the back of the figure fills the picture. · The statue is the painter's own marble group, brought to life as he kisses it."],[1,"/study/color-theory.html","Color Theory","Color Theory","","runge-farbenkugel","Modern color theory starts with Isaac Newton's Opticks (1704) and his prism. Goethe, the chemist Chevreul and the teacher Munsell then asked a painter's questions: how colors change beside each other, and how to name them exactly."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-comes-from-light.html","Color Theory","Color Comes from Light","","newton-opticks-circle","Color begins as light, and white sunlight already holds every hue. · The circle at right divides sunlight's colors into seven arcs: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet. · The arcs are unequal; Newton sized them to match the seven intervals of a musical scale. · The drawings at left trace light rays through a pair of glass prisms. · Claude Monet · Rouen Cathedral, West Façade · 1894 · Monet painted this front more than thirty times; here the stone comes out pale blue, lavender and peach, the colors of one light and weather. · A red apple absorbs most of the light that falls on it and returns mainly the red."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-comes-from-light.html","Color Theory · Example","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-rouen-west-facade","1894 · Monet painted this front more than thirty times; here the stone comes out pale blue, lavender and peach, the colors of one light and weather. · Color Comes from Light"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/mixing-light-and-mixing-paint.html","Color Theory","Mixing Light and Mixing Paint","","bezold-plate-i-mixtures","Mixing light adds colors together; mixing paint takes light away, so paint mixtures turn darker and duller. · Gamboge, a yellow, and Prussian blue each return a wide band of the spectrum, shown beside their swatches. · Stirred together as paint, each pigment swallows what the other returns, and only the green band they share is left. · Mixed as light, the same two keep both bands and come out a pale gray instead of green. · Titian · Bacchus and Ariadne · 1520–23 · Titian laid his pigments on nearly pure and lightened them only with white, so the ultramarine sky keeps the strength a mixture would lose."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/mixing-light-and-mixing-paint.html","Color Theory · Example","Bacchus and Ariadne","Titian","thumb/titian-bacchus-ariadne","1520–23 · Titian laid his pigments on nearly pure and lightened them only with white, so the ultramarine sky keeps the strength a mixture would lose. · Mixing Light and Mixing Paint"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/hue-value-and-saturation.html","Color Theory","Hue, Value and Saturation","","ostwald-farbenfibel-triangle","Any color can be pinned down by three things: its hue, its value (how light or dark it is) and its saturation (how strong or grayed it is). · The pure yellow sits at the left point; chips along the top edge take in white, those along the bottom take in black. · The one chip between the edges takes in both, and the yellow turns olive and dull. · The right-hand column loses the yellow altogether and runs from white to dark gray. · Sassoferrato · The Virgin in Prayer · 1640–50 · The mantle is one ultramarine blue throughout; only its value moves, from bright folds to deep navy shadow. · Painters also call saturation chroma or intensity."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/hue-value-and-saturation.html","Color Theory · Example","The Virgin in Prayer","Sassoferrato","thumb/sassoferrato-virgin-prayer","1640–50 · The mantle is one ultramarine blue throughout; only its value moves, from bright folds to deep navy shadow. · Hue, Value and Saturation"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-color-wheel.html","Color Theory","The Color Wheel","","chevreul-chromatic-circle","A color wheel bends the hues into a circle, so neighbors and opposites can be read at a glance. · Narrow wedges step around the circle from red through orange, yellow, green, blue and violet, and back to red. · Each mixed hue sits between the two it comes from: orange between red and yellow, green between yellow and blue. · Paul Signac · Evening Calm, Concarneau, Opus 220 (Allegro Maestoso) · 1891 · Signac took his shadows from across the wheel: the hill catches yellow-orange evening light, and the rocks fall into violet-blue. · Painters build the wheel from red, yellow and blue, yet no three real paints can mix every hue on it."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-color-wheel.html","Color Theory · Example","Evening Calm, Concarneau, Opus 220 (Allegro Maestoso)","Paul Signac","thumb/signac-evening-calm","1891 · Signac took his shadows from across the wheel: the hill catches yellow-orange evening light, and the rocks fall into violet-blue. · The Color Wheel"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-value-of-a-color.html","Color Theory","The Value of a Color","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","Each hue reaches its full strength at a lightness of its own. · The center column is a gray scale running from black at the bottom to white at the top. · Lines tie each color to the gray of equal lightness, from yellow near the top to purple near the bottom. · Red and blue meet the same gray step, so a black-and-white photograph would turn them about the same gray. · James McNeill Whistler · Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl · 1861–1863, 1872 · Whistler kept dress and curtain to one high, white value, so the red hair, the flowers and the blue carpet carry the few colors."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-value-of-a-color.html","Color Theory · Example","Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl","James McNeill Whistler","thumb/whistler-white-girl","1861–1863, 1872 · Whistler kept dress and curtain to one high, white value, so the red hair, the flowers and the blue carpet carry the few colors. · The Value of a Color"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/saturation.html","Color Theory","Saturation","","munsell-atlas-chroma-branches","Saturation is the distance between a color and a gray of the same lightness. · Each row of chips starts at the gray column and steps outward, every chip stronger than the one before. · The rows run to different lengths: red reaches farthest, while blue and purple stop well short. · Francisco de Goya · Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga · 1787–88 · Goya set one saturated red suit in a room of grays and browns, so the child is the strongest color in the picture. · Mixing in a gray of the same value dulls a color while keeping its lightness close to where it was."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/saturation.html","Color Theory · Example","Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga","Francisco de Goya","thumb/goya-red-boy","1787–88 · Goya set one saturated red suit in a room of grays and browns, so the child is the strongest color in the picture. · Saturation"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/warm-and-cool.html","Color Theory","Warm and Cool","","field-chromatography-fig-2","Reds, oranges and yellows read as warm and blues, greens and violets as cool, though any color is warmer or cooler only beside another. · An axis crosses the circle with Hot marked near the orange side and Cold near the blue. · A second axis marks advancing and retiring colors, the old studio idea that warm colors come forward. · Joseph Mallord William Turner · The Fighting Temeraire · 1839 · Turner splits the sky: a blazing orange sunset on the right, clear cool blue and a pale moon on the left. · Advancing warm color is only a tendency; on a light ground the effect can weaken or reverse."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/warm-and-cool.html","Color Theory · Example","The Fighting Temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner","thumb/turner-temeraire","1839 · Turner splits the sky: a blazing orange sunset on the right, clear cool blue and a pale moon on the left. · Warm and Cool"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/neutrals.html","Color Theory","Neutrals","","rood-1879-mixing-pigments","Neutrals are grays, browns and dull greens, and a painter can mix them from colors on opposite sides of the wheel. · The pure paints sit along the stepped edge; every other square mixes two of them. · Neighbors mix clean: chrome yellow with green gives a bright yellow-green. · Opposites mix dull: green with vermilion or crimson drops to brown and maroon. · Camille Corot · Ville-d'Avray · 1870 · Corot built the scene from gray-greens, dull browns and a pewter sky, so the pond's silver light becomes the brightest note. · Earth pigments such as ochre and umber come out of the ground already low in saturation."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/neutrals.html","Color Theory · Example","Ville-d'Avray","Camille Corot","thumb/corot-ville-davray","1870 · Corot built the scene from gray-greens, dull browns and a pewter sky, so the pond's silver light becomes the brightest note. · Neutrals"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/complementary-colors.html","Color Theory","Complementary Colors","","chevreul-1890-plate-i","Complementary colors sit directly across the wheel: red and green, orange and blue, yellow and violet. · Each disk is ringed with fine lines of its opposite: green round the red, red round the green. · Orange carries a blue ring, and blue a faint orange one. · Chevreul printed the rings to show the tint the eye adds by itself around a strong color. · Vincent van Gogh · The Night Café · 1888 · Van Gogh set blood-red walls against a green ceiling and billiard table, meaning the clash of opposites to feel harsh."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/complementary-colors.html","Color Theory · Example","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-night-cafe","1888 · Van Gogh set blood-red walls against a green ceiling and billiard table, meaning the clash of opposites to feel harsh. · Complementary Colors"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/monochromatic.html","Color Theory","Monochromatic","","chevreul-1890-plate-v","A monochromatic scheme uses a single hue, varied from light to dark. · One orange runs through a numbered scale, from 0, white, at the bottom to 21, black, at the top. · Each numbered step is only slightly darker than the one below it. · The middle steps carry the strongest orange; toward each end it pales or sinks. · James McNeill Whistler · Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach · 1870–75 · Whistler washed river, sky and barges in thin layers of one gray-blue and let a few small lights break the single hue."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/monochromatic.html","Color Theory · Example","Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach","James McNeill Whistler","thumb/whistler-battersea-reach","1870–75 · Whistler washed river, sky and barges in thin layers of one gray-blue and let a few small lights break the single hue. · Monochromatic"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/analogous-colors.html","Color Theory","Analogous Colors","","chevreul-1890-plate-vi-detail","Analogous colors are neighbors on the wheel, such as yellow, yellow-green and green, and they look calm together. · This half of Chevreul's circle runs from deep green at the top through yellow to orange at the bottom. · Any short run of these wedges, such as orange-yellow into orange, is an analogous set. · Small numbers on the rim count off even steps between the named hues. · Claude Monet · Water Lilies (Agapanthus) · c. 1915–26 · Monet kept the pond to violets, blues and greens, close neighbors, and let a few pale lily pads and warm blossoms break the run."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/analogous-colors.html","Color Theory · Example","Water Lilies (Agapanthus)","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-water-lilies-agapanthus","c. 1915–26 · Monet kept the pond to violets, blues and greens, close neighbors, and let a few pale lily pads and warm blossoms break the run. · Analogous Colors"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/triads.html","Color Theory","Triads","","bezold-plate-iv-triads","A triad is three colors spaced evenly around the wheel, such as red, yellow and blue, giving variety with balance. · Each disk is cut into three equal wedges, one color to a wedge. · Disk 9 holds the painter's red, yellow and blue; disk 10 a blue-green, an orange and a dark red-violet. · Johannes Vermeer · The Milkmaid · c. 1660 · Vermeer's kitchen rests on a triad, the yellow bodice, red skirt and deep blue apron and cloth, with blue clearly in charge."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/triads.html","Color Theory · Example","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-milkmaid","c. 1660 · Vermeer's kitchen rests on a triad, the yellow bodice, red skirt and deep blue apron and cloth, with blue clearly in charge. · Triads"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/contrast-of-area.html","Color Theory","Contrast of Area","","ct-extension","How much of each color a picture holds changes the balance: a small patch of a light or strong color can stand up to a large field. · Yellow, the lightest, needs only a quarter of the bar to hold its own against violet. · Orange takes a third against blue; red and green, alike in lightness, split the bar evenly. · The darker the partner, the more room it is given. · Camille Corot · River with a Distant Tower · 1865 · Corot gives most of the canvas to pale sky, silvery greens and gray water, then adds a few touches of red on the figures that the eye finds at once. · The ratios probably trace back to brightness values Arthur Schopenhauer gave in 1816, though Itten credited Goethe; treat them as rough guides."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/contrast-of-area.html","Color Theory · Example","River with a Distant Tower","Camille Corot","thumb/corot-river-tower","1865 · Corot gives most of the canvas to pale sky, silvery greens and gray water, then adds a few touches of red on the figures that the eye finds at once. · Contrast of Area"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/simultaneous-contrast.html","Color Theory","Simultaneous Contrast","","chevreul-1890-plate-ii","A color changes with what surrounds it, so the same paint looks different on different grounds. · One ornament, printed in a single light ink, sits on six colored grounds. · On the yellow ground it almost disappears; on the dark red it stands out nearly white. · Vincent van Gogh · Irises · 1889 · Van Gogh set blue-violet irises against orange flowers and green leaves against red earth, so each color makes its neighbor look stronger. · Chevreul listed the shifts: gray patterns look greenish on rose, pink on green, lilac on yellow and orange-gray on blue."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/simultaneous-contrast.html","Color Theory · Example","Irises","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-irises-getty","1889 · Van Gogh set blue-violet irises against orange flowers and green leaves against red earth, so each color makes its neighbor look stronger. · Simultaneous Contrast"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/optical-mixing.html","Color Theory","Optical Mixing","","chevreul-1890-plate-x","Small touches of two colors placed side by side blend in the eye into a third color without being mixed as paint. · Each band is printed as fine stripes of two colors laid side by side, never blended. · Red and yellow threads read as orange in the top band; red and blue read as a dark violet in the middle one. · Yellow threads scattered through blue turn the left end of the bottom band green. · Henri-Edmond Cross · Pines Along the Shore · 1896 · Cross laid the shore in separate dabs of orange, pink and violet; from a step back they merge into warm, sunlit ground. · Chevreul had the Gobelins tapestry workshop weave such samples, and found that threads of opposite colors cancel toward gray instead."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/optical-mixing.html","Color Theory · Example","Pines Along the Shore","Henri-Edmond Cross","thumb/cross-pines-shore","1896 · Cross laid the shore in separate dabs of orange, pink and violet; from a step back they merge into warm, sunlit ground. · Optical Mixing"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/local-and-expressive-color.html","Color Theory","Local and Expressive Color","","ct-local-expressive","Local color is the color a thing has in plain daylight; expressive color is chosen for feeling or design instead. · The left panel gives sky, hill and tree their everyday colors. · The middle panel keeps the forms but tints everything with sunset light, the way the eye sees it at that hour. · The right panel invents a green sky, pink hill and yellow tree that no daylight would give. · Paul Gauguin · Mahana no atua (Day of the God) · 1894 · Gauguin painted this in Paris from memory and fantasy, so the bank turns pink and the water breaks into flat zones of yellow, blue and violet."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/local-and-expressive-color.html","Color Theory · Example","Mahana no atua (Day of the God)","Paul Gauguin","thumb/gauguin-day-of-god","1894 · Gauguin painted this in Paris from memory and fantasy, so the bank turns pink and the water breaks into flat zones of yellow, blue and violet. · Local and Expressive Color"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-and-emotion.html","Color Theory","Color and Emotion","","goethe-schiller-temperamentenrose","Colors carry mood: strong, warm colors tend to read as lively or tense and cool, dull ones as quiet or sad, though viewers differ. · Goethe and Schiller divided a ring of colors among the four temperaments, written around the rim. · Orange and orange-red fall to the choleric temperament, blue to the phlegmatic. · An inner ring pairs each color with a kind of person, from tyrants and heroes to poets and teachers. · Edvard Munch · The Scream · 1893 · Munch's sky is a band of blood-red and orange over a blue-black fjord; the colors carry the alarm as much as the figure does."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-and-emotion.html","Color Theory · Example","The Scream","Edvard Munch","thumb/munch-scream","1893 · Munch's sky is a band of blood-red and orange over a blue-black fjord; the colors carry the alarm as much as the figure does. · Color and Emotion"],[2,"/study/composition.html","Composition","Composition","","poore-picture-vision","Composition is how a painter arranges the parts of a picture. In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti named it one of three parts of a painter's job and compared the picture to an open window. In his 1899 book Composition, the American teacher Arthur Wesley Dow drew heavily on Japanese art, with line, dark-and-light and color as its elements."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/format.html","Composition","Format","","dow-three-proportions","Format is the outline of the whole picture, tall, wide, square or round, and one subject feels different in each. · One house and one tree are redrawn in three frames: tall and narrow, nearly square, and a wider upright. · The narrow version lets the tree dominate, with bare ground filling most of its lower half. · In the two squarer frames the house claims far more of the space. · Arthur Wesley Dow · View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River · ca. 1895 · The Met links this tiny upright print to Japanese pillar prints; its narrow frame stacks the view in bands, from field to houses to river to hills. · A picture is also a flat surface, and its pattern of shapes can pull against the illusion of space, says Smarthistory's Steven Zucker."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/format.html","Composition · Example","View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River","Arthur Wesley Dow","thumb/dow-view-of-ipswich","ca. 1895 · The Met links this tiny upright print to Japanese pillar prints; its narrow frame stacks the view in bands, from field to houses to river to hills. · Format"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/cropping.html","Composition","Cropping","","dow-rectangles-various-shape","Cropping decides which parts of a scene the frame keeps, and where its edges slice through what remains. · One stand of trees is framed five ways: one nearly square, two tall and two wide. · Each frame cuts the trunks and treetops at a different place, so each makes a different picture. · Edgar Degas · A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?) · 1865 · The bouquet takes the center while the woman is pushed against the right edge and cut off by it; a Met curator argues Degas planned such layouts to look like chance. · The Met notes that Degas took bold cropping from Japanese prints, and from sixteenth-century painters who sometimes left a figure partly outside the picture."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/cropping.html","Composition · Example","A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?)","Edgar Degas","thumb/degas-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers","1865 · The bouquet takes the center while the woman is pushed against the right edge and cut off by it; a Met curator argues Degas planned such layouts to look like chance. · Cropping"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/positive-and-negative-space.html","Composition","Positive and Negative Space","","dow-notan-reversal","Positive space is the subject of a picture; negative space is everything around the subject, and it has a shape of its own. · One round design is printed three ways, with its blacks and whites traded around. · On the left the black center and the white petals both read as shapes. · On the right the center turns white, set inside dark petals and a black ring. · Félix Vallotton · Laziness (La Paresse) · 1896 · Vallotton uses no gray at all; the pale body is mostly bare paper, given its outline by the black around it."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/positive-and-negative-space.html","Composition · Example","Laziness (La Paresse)","Félix Vallotton","thumb/vallotton-laziness","1896 · Vallotton uses no gray at all; the pale body is mostly bare paper, given its outline by the black around it. · Positive and Negative Space"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/value-massing.html","Composition","Value Massing","","dow-notan-spotting","Value massing groups the light and dark parts of a picture into a few big shapes, so the design reads at a glance; Dow called this notan, Japanese for \"dark-light.\" · Corot's landscape at lower right is one great dark mass of tree with a pale gap of sky below it. · Manet's beach at top left is the reverse: a pale field holding one large dark shape. · Katsushika Hokusai · Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave) · ca. 1830–32 · Seen as dark and light, the print comes down to a few big shapes: the deep blue body of the wave, the pale sky, and Fuji's white peak against a band of gray. · To Dow, notan was a pattern the painter designs, separate from the light and shadow that fall on things in nature."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/value-massing.html","Composition · Example","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","thumb/hokusai-under-the-wave-off-kanagawa","ca. 1830–32 · Seen as dark and light, the print comes down to a few big shapes: the deep blue body of the wave, the pale sky, and Fuji's white peak against a band of gray. · Value Massing"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/focal-point.html","Composition","Focal Point","","poore-lion-desert","The focal point is where the eye lands first, and painters build it with contrast, isolation and placement. · A single dark lion lies alone in a pale, nearly empty desert. · The other darks, a low strip on the horizon and a few scrubby plants at lower right, are thin and broken by comparison. · Caravaggio · The Denial of Saint Peter · 1610 · Lit faces and hands stand out against near-black darkness, and the Met reads the pointing fingers as three accusations, matching Peter's three denials. · The English artist J. T. Smith advised in 1797 that a picture should never hold two equal main lights: one should lead and the rest give way."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/focal-point.html","Composition · Example","The Denial of Saint Peter","Caravaggio","thumb/caravaggio-denial-of-saint-peter","1610 · Lit faces and hands stand out against near-black darkness, and the Met reads the pointing fingers as three accusations, matching Peter's three denials. · Focal Point"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/symmetric-balance.html","Composition","Symmetric Balance","","poore-salute-wounded","In symmetric balance, the two sides of a center line carry about the same weight, often by mirroring each other. · A ruled vertical splits the scene into two groups of about the same size. · Officers on horseback at left face the column of men at right across the line. · Raphael · Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece) · ca. 1504 · The Virgin sits on the center line with a female and a male saint on each side, and God the Father is centered again in the arch above. · Dow saw symmetry as the plainest answer to a wish for order, but noted it had come to be used in \"cheap and mean design\" with no regard for beauty of form."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/symmetric-balance.html","Composition · Example","Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece)","Raphael","thumb/raphael-colonna-altarpiece","ca. 1504 · The Virgin sits on the center line with a female and a male saint on each side, and God the Father is centered again in the arch above. · Symmetric Balance"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/asymmetric-balance.html","Composition","Asymmetric Balance","","poore-steelyard","Asymmetric balance pairs two unlike sides that still feel level. · The drawings run from two equal pans, to one heavy weight against a small one, to a seesaw. · On the seesaw a large man sits near the pivot while a small child is raised high at the far end. · Mary Cassatt · Little Girl in a Blue Armchair · 1878 · The large chair holding the sprawled girl sits right of center; the small dark dog in its chair and a stretch of open floor answer them at left. · Poore held that no part of a picture is weightless; even a blank area pulls a little."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/asymmetric-balance.html","Composition · Example","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","thumb/cassatt-little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair","1878 · The large chair holding the sprawled girl sits right of center; the small dark dog in its chair and a stretch of open floor answer them at left. · Asymmetric Balance"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/armatures.html","Composition","Armatures","","poore-fundamental-forms","An armature is a basic figure, such as a triangle, a circle, an S-curve, a cross or an L, that the main masses of a picture are built around. · Under each emblem along the top, small sketches show pictures built on that shape. · In the landscape row the triangle lies on its side, its point running away into the distance along a road. · Andrea del Sarto · The Holy Family with the Young Saint John the Baptist · ca. 1528 · The Met notes how the four figures' hands spiral around the globe that John passes to the Christ Child; the group gathers around that one point. · Poore counted six basic forms and thought only the L, his rectangle, needs a counterweight on the far side."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/armatures.html","Composition · Example","The Holy Family with the Young Saint John the Baptist","Andrea del Sarto","thumb/sarto-holy-family","ca. 1528 · The Met notes how the four figures' hands spiral around the globe that John passes to the Christ Child; the group gathers around that one point. · Armatures"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/thirds-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Composition","Thirds and the Golden Ratio","","poore-strong-points","Placement rules, such as the rule of thirds and the golden ratio, claim that some spots in a frame are stronger than others; the evidence for them is thin. · Unequal lines divide the frame, and the tree trunk stands on one of them, left of center. · The horizon runs along another line, a little above the middle. · Georges Seurat · Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque) · 1887–88 · Rows of stiff horizontals and verticals invite golden-ratio overlays, yet Roger Fischler's study of Seurat's notes, sketches and paintings, summarized in 1981, found no sign he used the ratio. · A 2014 study of 727 paintings found the rule of thirds plays at most a minor role, and art historian Martin Kemp has found no Renaissance drawing built on a hidden golden ratio."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/thirds-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Composition · Example","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-circus-sideshow","1887–88 · Rows of stiff horizontals and verticals invite golden-ratio overlays, yet Roger Fischler's study of Seurat's notes, sketches and paintings, summarized in 1981, found no sign he used the ratio. · Thirds and the Golden Ratio"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/line-and-direction.html","Composition","Line and Direction","","poore-aesthetics-of-line","Every line, edge or gesture pushes the eye along it, and the direction it takes carries a mood. · Tall trunks fill the forest scene at top left, while a flock spreads along a low horizon in the middle. · At lower left a ring of dancers circles in a loop; at lower right swallows dive on a slant. · Jacques Louis David · The Death of Socrates · 1787 · Socrates' followers are lined up to either side along the couch in one flat, level band, and his bent arm rises out of it. · The Getty calls this movement: the route the eye travels through a work, often toward its focal areas."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/line-and-direction.html","Composition · Example","The Death of Socrates","Jacques Louis David","thumb/david-death-of-socrates","1787 · Socrates' followers are lined up to either side along the couch in one flat, level band, and his bent arm rises out of it. · Line and Direction"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/rhythm.html","Composition","Rhythm","","dow-repetition","Rhythm comes from repeating a shape or line, and the spacing between the repeats sets its beat. · Across the top border, one stepped unit of black and white repeats at equal intervals. · Below, farmhouse gables repeat across a landscape at uneven intervals and in different sizes. · Utagawa Hiroshige · Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake · 1857 · Fine parallel rain lines cover the whole print, and beneath the deck the bridge's posts make a slower, heavier repeat. · Dow thought repetition likely the most ancient form of design; the Getty adds that some variety keeps a rhythm lively."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/rhythm.html","Composition · Example","Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake","Utagawa Hiroshige","thumb/hiroshige-sudden-shower","1857 · Fine parallel rain lines cover the whole print, and beneath the deck the bridge's posts make a slower, heavier repeat. · Rhythm"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/leading-the-eye.html","Composition","Leading the Eye","","poore-shepherdess-exit","Painters plan where the eye comes into a picture and where it goes out. · A row of trees runs across the picture, with the shepherdess leaning on one of them. · Pale sky opens behind the shepherdess and again beyond the trees at right, drawing the eye out in two directions. · Johannes Vermeer · Young Woman with a Water Pitcher · ca. 1662 · Window light falls on the white headdress and collar and on the pitcher and basin; in the Met's audio guide, a speaker says the light \"is guiding me around the room.\" · Poore counted two ways out like these a fault; a 2020 eye-tracking study found composition does draw the eye to the chief elements, though the order of looking varies."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/leading-the-eye.html","Composition · Example","Young Woman with a Water Pitcher","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher","ca. 1662 · Window light falls on the white headdress and collar and on the pitcher and basin; in the Met's audio guide, a speaker says the light \"is guiding me around the room.\" · Leading the Eye"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/depth.html","Composition","Depth","","dow-depth-hiroshige","Depth cues, such as overlap and linear perspective, make a flat surface look deep. · Two huge gate posts stand in front and cut across everything behind them. · Two walkers by the building are drawn large; the figures far back at right are small. · Utagawa Hiroshige · Plum Estate, Kameido · 1857 · The plum trunk is so close it runs off the frame, and the orchard and its small visitors are seen through its branches, with almost nothing in between. · Linear perspective, worked out by Brunelleschi around 1420 and explained by Alberti, runs receding lines to one vanishing point level with the viewer's eye."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/depth.html","Composition · Example","Plum Estate, Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","thumb/hiroshige-plum-estate-kameido","1857 · The plum trunk is so close it runs off the frame, and the orchard and its small visitors are seen through its branches, with almost nothing in between. · Depth"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/unity-and-variety.html","Composition","Unity and Variety","","poore-unity","Unity is the feeling that all the parts make one whole; variety adds enough difference to hold attention. · Above, two figures press into one tight embrace, so the picture has a single center. · Below, two seated figures each hold their own bird, and the picture splits into two halves. · Georges Seurat · A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884 · 1884–86 · Dozens of different figures are tied together by repeats: upright profiles, parasols, curved hats. Seurat's aim, the Art Institute says, was a modern-life painting to match a classical Greek frieze. · Poore ranked unity, with balance, above every other principle of composition."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/unity-and-variety.html","Composition · Example","A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-grande-jatte","1884–86 · Dozens of different figures are tied together by repeats: upright profiles, parasols, curved hats. Seurat's aim, the Art Institute says, was a modern-life painting to match a classical Greek frieze. · Unity and Variety"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/proportion-and-scale.html","Composition","Proportion and Scale","","dow-rectangles-proportion","Proportion is how the sizes of a picture's parts relate to one another, including how big a thing looks beside what is around it. · The tracings show rows of arched windows, stacked panels, and a wide opening beside narrow shelves. · In the three shelf layouts at the bottom, each square is cut into parts of different sizes. · Thomas Cole · View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow · 1836 · Cole painted himself as a tiny figure near the bottom, looking up from his sketch, and beside that speck the valley reads as vast. · A painting's own size matters too: a big canvas pushes viewers back, while a small one draws them close."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/proportion-and-scale.html","Composition · Example","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","thumb/cole-oxbow","1836 · Cole painted himself as a tiny figure near the bottom, looking up from his sketch, and beside that speck the valley reads as vast. · Proportion and Scale"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/checking-a-composition.html","Composition","Checking a Composition","","dow-two-value-sketches","To judge a design, painters make it look strange to their own eyes: shrunk, mirrored, turned upside down or seen from across the room. · Boats, trees, a road and a house are each set down in a few brushstrokes. · Trees become solid blots with ragged edges, and water is a few short dashes. · Georges Seurat · Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\" · 1884 · Seurat painted more than 20 studies like this small panel to try out the big canvas's layout; the Art Institute notes the three figures at right were completely rethought. · Leonardo advised a mirror, where a painting looks like another painter's work; neuroscientist Margaret Livingstone suggests such tricks work by stripping out depth, leaving flat shapes."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/checking-a-composition.html","Composition · Example","Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\"","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-oil-sketch-grande-jatte","1884 · Seurat painted more than 20 studies like this small panel to try out the big canvas's layout; the Art Institute notes the three figures at right were completely rethought. · Checking a Composition"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Colour let loose, then line and paint pushed to the edge · In 1905 a group of painters in Paris, soon nicknamed the Fauves, the wild beasts, began using colour for its own force rather than to describe things. Within a few years painters in Dresden, Munich and Vienna pushed the same freedom further, into jagged line, raw paint and, by 1913, pictures with no objects at all. · Henri Matisse · Woman with a Hat · 1905"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/woman-with-a-hat.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Woman with a Hat","Henri Matisse","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 31 3/4 × 23 1/2 in. San Francisco Museum of Modern Art · Henri Matisse (French, 1869–1954). The face is built from patches of green, mauve, yellow and orange, with only a few touches the colour of skin. · A green stroke runs down the nose and along the jaw, with pink and yellow patches beside it. · The hat is a heap of blue, red, green and violet dabs that does not settle into any one object. · The background is scrubbed in loose strokes of turquoise, pink and green, with the pale ground showing through. · The dress and glove are painted as quickly as the background, so figure and setting carry the same weight. · Shown at the Salon d'Automne in Paris in 1905, the exhibition where the nickname was coined; the sitter is the artist's wife, Amélie."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-madame-matisse-the-green-line.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of Madame Matisse. The Green Line","Henri Matisse","matisse-the-green-line","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 16 × 12 3/4 in. Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen · A single stripe of green runs down the middle of the face and splits it into a warm yellow half and a cool pink half. · The green line runs from the hairline down the nose to the chin, and the yellow and pink halves meet along it without blending. · The background is cut into three flat fields: violet, orange-red and green, each meeting the next with a hard edge. · The hair is one dark blue mass with a few lighter strokes, not drawn strand by strand. · Thick strokes cover the whole surface, the background laid on as heavily as the face. · The museum dates it, with a 'presumably', to autumn 1905, after the summer at Collioure."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/open-window-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Open Window, Collioure","Henri Matisse","matisse-open-window-collioure","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 18 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The paint is laid on in loose, separate strokes, and bare canvas shows between them across much of the picture. · The view through the window is a patchwork of short pink, blue and white dabs for sea, sky and boats. · The left wall is green and the right wall is pink, two sides of the same room in opposite colours. · The window frames are striped in red, green and blue rather than painted as wood. · The flowerpots and leaves at the bottom are blobs of red, green and blue, each one or two strokes. · Shown at the 1905 Salon d'Automne; the view is of sailboats off Collioure, a town on the Mediterranean coast."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/mountains-at-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Mountains at Collioure","André Derain","derain-mountains-at-collioure","André Derain · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 39 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · André Derain (French, 1880–1954). The trees are made of separate dashes of pure blue, green and yellow, laid side by side without touching. · Each dash is one flat colour, so the foliage reads as a mosaic. · The tree trunks are bright red-orange lines, and the hills behind are flat zones of orange, blue and violet. · The sky is pale green and yellow with white clouds drawn in long curving strokes. · Nothing is shaded: light and shadow are replaced by changes of colour. · Derain and Matisse worked side by side at Collioure in summer 1905."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/le-bonheur-de-vivre.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Le Bonheur de vivre","Henri Matisse","matisse-joy-of-life","Henri Matisse · 1905–06 · Oil on canvas, 69 1/2 × 94 3/4 in. Barnes Foundation, Philadelphia · The figures and trees are flat areas of colour held inside long curving outlines, with almost no modelling. · The reclining women are pale pink shapes edged in red or green. · Tree trunks bend in sweeping arcs, and the foliage above is a set of flat green, pink and orange patches. · The ground shifts from yellow to orange to green in broad zones, each colour laid flat. · A ring of small dancers in the distance is painted as a loop of outlines on the yellow field. · Painted between October 1905 and March 1906 and shown at the 1906 Salon des Indépendants; bought there by Leo Stein."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/street-dresden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Street, Dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-street-dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1908, reworked 1919 · Oil on canvas, 59 1/4 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Ernst Ludwig Kirchner (German, 1880–1938). The street is a field of acid pink, and the crowd presses to the front edge with green and yellow faces. · The pavement is a flat pink that tilts steeply upward, so there is no sense of a receding street. · The woman in the pale yellow blouse has a green, masklike face, and both women in front are rimmed with dark outlines around hats and shoulders. · Colours clash: orange coat against pink ground, lemon-yellow blouse against dark blue skirt. · The figures at the sides are cut off by the edges of the canvas, as if the crowd continued past the frame. · Kirchner helped found Die Brücke (The Bridge), a group of German painters, in Dresden in 1905; the street is the Königstrasse, and the canvas carries a date of 1907, a year before it was painted."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/franzi-in-front-of-carved-chair.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Fränzi in Front of Carved Chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-franzi-carved-chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 28 × 19 1/2 in. Museo Nacional Thyssen-Bornemisza, Madrid · The face is bright green with red lips, drawn in a few jagged dark strokes, and the chair behind is a black shape with a pink face in it. · The face is a flat yellow-green with blue around the eyes and pink patches on the cheeks and nose. · Outlines are thick, dark and angular, laid down fast, with the raw canvas showing at the edges. · The pink carved figure in the chair back sits directly above the girl's head, a second, smaller face stacked on top of hers. · The background is split into blocks of yellow, green and blue with no depth. · Fränzi was a girl from a working-class district of Dresden who posed for the Brücke painters; the chair was most likely carved by Kirchner himself."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/english-step-dancers.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","English Step Dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-english-step-dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1911 · Colour woodcut, image 16 × 10 3/4 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The lines are cut in a wood block, so every contour is stiff and splintery, and the colour comes in three flat inks printed with the black. · The black lines are thick, straight-sided and broken, the marks of a knife in wood rather than a brush. · Pink, green and ochre are printed as flat areas with no shading, and the pink sits slightly off the black. · The dancers' legs are long and angular, with the stripes of their tights cut as parallel gouges. · The plant behind is simplified to a few cut leaves, each a single shape. · Printed by the artist himself, as the inscription says; given to the Jena art society in 1918, seized from it in 1937 as 'degenerate art', and in New York by 1940."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-as-a-soldier.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait as a Soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-self-portrait-as-soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 27 1/4 × 25 in. Allen Memorial Art Museum, Oberlin College, Oberlin, Ohio · The face and uniform are cut into sharp planes, and the paint is dragged in hatched strokes that leave every edge hard. · The face is a yellow-ochre wedge with sunken blue eyes and a hard shadow down the nose. · The right sleeve ends in a flat red stump with no hand. · Blue uniform, orange wall and yellow skin are set side by side at full strength, with no blending. · The nude behind is painted in the same ochre as the face, so the two figures read as one colour against the dark ground. · Kirchner was inducted in 1915 and released for illness; in 1937 the painting hung in the Munich exhibition the Nazis called Degenerate Art."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/murnau-view-from-the-window-of-the-griesbrau.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Murnau – View from the Window of the Griesbräu","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-murnau-griesbraeu","Wassily Kandinsky · 1908 · Oil on cardboard, 19 1/2 × 27 1/2 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Wassily Kandinsky (Russian, 1866–1944). The village is melting into blocks of colour: the road is violet and pink, one roof is orange and the fields are flat green. · The road is a wide band of mauve and pink strokes filling the centre of the picture, with two tiny figures the only detail on it. · The houses are simplified to boxes of white, blue and ochre with the windows as short dark strokes. · Long brushstrokes run in one direction across each surface, giving every patch a visible grain. · The hills at the top are a thin band of dark blue and violet, with a thinner strip of yellow sky above them. · Painted from the window of the inn where Kandinsky and his friends lodged in summer 1908; the museum describes that stay as the moment their painting broke loose into free, expressive colour."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-the-dancer-alexander-sakharoff.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of the Dancer Alexander Sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky","jawlensky-alexander-sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky · 1909 · Oil on cardboard, 27 1/4 × 26 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Alexej von Jawlensky (Russian, 1864–1941). A thick dark contour wraps the red costume and black hair, and inside the lines the colour is flat. · The red costume is a single flat area, broken only by the darker red rose and green leaf at the chest and a dark blue shape at the bottom edge. · The face is chalk white with black-rimmed eyes and a red mouth, like stage make-up, and the eyes stare straight out. · The background is pale green brushed loosely, with brown cardboard showing through at the left. · The pose is frontal and symmetrical, head centred, shoulders square. · The museum records that Jawlensky painted it in less than thirty minutes when the dancer visited his studio in costume before a performance."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/blue-horse-i.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Blue Horse I","Franz Marc","marc-blue-horse-i","Franz Marc · 1911 · Oil on canvas, 44 × 33 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Franz Marc (German, 1880–1916). The horse is blue from mane to hoof, standing in hills of red, yellow and green that are just as unreal. · The animal is modelled in shades of blue and white, with a dark blue mane and a rounded, simplified body. · The landscape behind is a set of curved colour bands: pink sky, blue and violet hills, a yellow field, a red slope. · Complementary pairs sit next to each other: red against green at the bottom, blue horse against yellow ground. · Dark green leaves at the lower right are the sharpest shapes in a picture of soft curves. · Marc wrote to Macke in December 1910 that blue stood for the male principle, yellow for the female, red for matter; the museum says this picture shows that colour theory unusually clearly."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/lady-in-a-green-jacket.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Lady in a Green Jacket","August Macke","macke-lady-green-jacket","August Macke · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 17 1/4 × 17 in. Museum Ludwig, Cologne · August Macke (German, 1887–1914). The figures are smooth, rounded colour shapes with no faces, set in a landscape of equally smooth blocks. · The woman in green is seen from behind, her jacket one curved green form over a dark skirt. · The couple at the left and the woman in violet at the right are painted the same way, as coloured silhouettes. · The lake is a flat blue band, the path and houses are yellow and ochre blocks, and the trees are circles of green. · The surface is brushed in small, even strokes, so even the brightest colours stay calm. · Bought in 1947 for the Wallraf-Richartz-Museum in Cologne and transferred to the Museum Ludwig in 1976."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/improvisation-no-30-cannons.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Improvisation No. 30 (Cannons)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-improvisation-30-cannons","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 43 3/4 × 43 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · A few things can still be picked out, two cannons at the lower right, leaning towers at the top, but most of the canvas is colour and line with nothing to name. · Two black cannon barrels tilt up from the lower right corner, the clearest objects in the picture. · A cluster of tall buildings with a red tower leans at the upper right, drawn in thin dark lines over colour. · A big blue shape with a red outline runs diagonally through the centre, attached to nothing. · The colours are soft and watery in places, thick in others, and spread in blotches rather than filling shapes. · First shown in London in 1913; Kandinsky wrote to the collector Arthur Jerome Eddy that the cannons might reflect all the talk of war that year, but that the real content was what the viewer felt in the forms and colours."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/composition-vii.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Composition VII","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-composition-vii","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, about 79 × 118 in. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · Almost nothing can be named: the whole canvas is a storm of colour patches and black lines that pull toward the centre. · A dark, dense knot of black strokes and blue sits left of centre, and everything around it is lighter and looser. · Colour runs in every direction, with red, yellow, blue and green all at full strength and no single colour in charge. · Black lines, some thin and some thick, curve and break across the colours without enclosing them. · The edges are quieter, with large soft areas of cream, so the eye is pushed back toward the middle. · Painted in four days, 25 to 28 November 1913, after more than thirty studies; the museum notes faint echoes of Kandinsky's earlier pictures of the Apocalypse."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-laughing.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait, Laughing","Richard Gerstl","gerstl-laughing-self-portrait","Richard Gerstl · 1908 · Oil on canvas, 15 3/4 × 12 in. Belvedere, Vienna · Richard Gerstl (Austrian, 1883–1908). The paint is smeared and dabbed so thickly that the face is half dissolved into the brown dots behind it. · The background is a field of brown, ochre and green dabs, each a separate thick touch. · The face is smeared rather than dabbed, and its edges blur into the background at the right cheek and chin. · The open mouth and the two eyes are the only sharply drawn passages. · The jacket is a few dark smears at the bottom, barely indicated. · Gerstl taught himself to paint, kept apart from Vienna's artist groups and died at twenty-five; the museum reads this laugh as more menacing than cheerful."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-with-chinese-lantern-plant.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait with Chinese Lantern Plant","Egon Schiele","schiele-self-portrait-physalis","Egon Schiele · 1912 · Oil and opaque colour on wood, 12 3/4 × 15 3/4 in. Leopold Museum, Vienna · Egon Schiele (Austrian, 1890–1918). A hard, wiry contour runs around the head and shoulders, set against an almost empty pale ground. · The face is drawn with a fine dark line and shaded in streaks of grey, green and red, so the skin looks bruised rather than lit. · The head tilts one way and the eyes look the other, straight at the viewer. · The background is flat off-white with the brushstrokes showing, and the red lantern fruits on the left are the only bright colour. · The top edge cuts through the hair and the bottom edge through the chest, so the figure is cropped close. · Painted as a pair with Schiele's portrait of Wally Neuzil, which the museum says accounts for the odd framing of both pictures."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/death-and-maiden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Death and Maiden","Egon Schiele","schiele-death-and-maiden","Egon Schiele · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 59 × 70 3/4 in. Belvedere, Vienna · The sheet and the ground are broken into jagged, crumpled shapes, and the dark robe and the ground share cracked earth colours. · The white sheet is a mass of sharp folds, each edge drawn with a dark line. · The woman's dress is a patchwork of red, pink and white marks, like a quilt of small splinters. · The ground behind is a field of brown and ochre lumps, with no horizon or sky except a thin pale strip at the top. · The two figures lie at an angle across the picture, and their poses look unstable, as if the embrace could slip apart. · The man is Schiele himself and the woman holding on to him is Wally Neuzil, whom he left to marry Edith Harms."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","monet-impression","Impressionism is a way of painting that took shape in and around Paris from the late 1860s. Its painters first showed together in 1874, in an exhibition they organized themselves. · Claude Monet · Impression, Sunrise · 1872"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/olympia.html","Impressionism","Olympia","Édouard Manet","manet-olympia","Édouard Manet · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 51 1/2 × 75 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Édouard Manet (French, 1832–1883). Look at how little shading there is on the body: Manet paints it as a pale, nearly flat shape. · Light falls straight from the front, so there are almost no shadows. · The flowers and the shawl are quick, loose touches of color. · The whites of the sheets and pillows are laid on in broad strokes. · The dark green and brown background pushes the pale figure forward. · First shown at the Salon of 1865, the state-run exhibition in Paris, where it caused a scandal."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-grenouillere.html","Impressionism","La Grenouillère","Claude Monet","monet-grenouillere","Claude Monet · 1869 · Oil on canvas, 29 1/4 × 39 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Claude Monet (French, 1840–1926). Look at the water: it is built from separate strokes, not smoothly blended paint. · Short, curved strokes of blue, white, green and black sit side by side across the water. · Each stroke stays distinct, so the water seems to move and catch the light. · The figures on the round island are a few dark dabs each. · The trees behind are broad patches of yellow-green, with little detail. · Monet painted it at a riverside bathing spot near Paris, working beside Renoir."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/impression-sunrise.html","Impressionism","Impression, Sunrise","Claude Monet","monet-impression","Claude Monet · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 19 3/4 × 25 1/2 in. Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris · Look at how thin and quick the paint is: Monet painted it in a few hours. · Thin, watery paint lets the weave of the canvas show through in the upper sky. · The nearest boat is a single dark shape, with no detail. · The orange sun and its broken reflection are the brightest marks; orange streaks in the sky echo them. · Ships and cranes behind are faint blue-gray shapes in the haze. · Shown at the 1874 exhibition. A critic used its title to mock the group, and the name stuck."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-cradle.html","Impressionism","The Cradle","Berthe Morisot","morisot-cradle","Berthe Morisot · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 22 × 18 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Berthe Morisot (French, 1841–1895). Look at the veil over the cradle: it is painted in thin, see-through strokes. · Most of the canvas is white and pale pink, laid on in loose, open strokes. · The veil's edge runs on a diagonal from the woman's hand up to the top right. · Her dark blue dress and the dark wall at the top right frame the pale cradle. · Soft edges, more than outlines, separate the cradle from the curtains behind. · The sitter is Morisot's sister Edma, watching her baby daughter."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/white-frost.html","Impressionism","White Frost","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-hoarfrost","Camille Pissarro · 1873 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 36 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Camille Pissarro (French, 1830–1903). Look at the long tree shadows on the frosty field: they are blue, not brown or black. · The sunlit ground between the shadows is warm orange and tan. · Warm light next to cool shadow makes the field look cold and bright. · Small, thick dabs cover the furrows, giving the whole field an even texture. · Shown at the 1874 exhibition."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/boulevard-des-capucines.html","Impressionism","Boulevard des Capucines","Claude Monet","monet-capucines","Claude Monet · 1873–74 · Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 23 3/4 in. The Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City · Look down at the crowd: Monet paints each person with one or two strokes. · The street tilts up, so the pavement fills the lower half. · The bare trees are a haze of thin gray-violet strokes. · A cluster of pink balloons at the right is the brightest spot of warm color in a cool picture. · Monet painted this view twice; the version shown in 1874 was probably the other one."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-loge.html","Impressionism","La Loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 25 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Pierre-Auguste Renoir (French, 1841–1919). Look at the black stripes of the dress: black is used as a strong color, not just for shadow. · Black stripes and white silk make the strongest contrast in the picture. · The blacks are brushed with visible strokes, so they look soft, not flat. · The woman's face is smooth and clear, while the man behind her is looser and darker. · Pink flowers at her chest and in her hair add soft touches of color. · The models were Renoir's brother Edmond and a model known as Nini."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-dance-class.html","Impressionism","The Dance Class","Edgar Degas","degas-dance-class","Edgar Degas · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 32 3/4 × 30 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Edgar Degas (French, 1834–1917). Look at the empty floor at the lower right: the dancers are pushed to the left, the back and the right edge. · An empty stretch of floorboards runs from the lower right back into the room. · Figures crowd the left and right, and some are cut off by the frame. · A double bass and a music stand at the lower left cut into the view. · The white skirts are light, feathery strokes against the green wall. · The teacher leaning on a stick is the ballet master Jules Perrot."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/bal-du-moulin-de-la-galette.html","Impressionism","Bal du moulin de la Galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 51 3/4 × 69 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Look at the spots of light on the dancers: sun through the leaves is painted as loose, pale patches. · The patches cross the figures without following their shapes, so light and figure blend. · Dark blue jackets and hats make strong shapes among the pale patches. · Strokes of blue, pink and yellow repeat across the whole crowd, tying it together. · Some of Renoir's friends posed for it."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/paris-street-rainy-day.html","Impressionism","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 83 1/2 × 108 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Gustave Caillebotte (French, 1848–1894). Look at the deep space: a new Paris street runs far back, with figures close up at the front. · Building edges and paving stones run back to distant points, so the space feels deep. · A green lamppost splits the canvas into two halves. · The couple at the right is close and large; people across the square are tiny. · The paint is smooth and finely blended, like the finish taught in art schools. · It was the centerpiece of the group's 1877 exhibition, which Caillebotte did much to organize."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Impressionism","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/4 × 51 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Mary Cassatt (American, 1844–1926). Look at the big blue shapes of the chairs: they fill the room and crowd the girl. · Blue armchairs and a sofa make large, rounded shapes across the canvas. · The gray floor is a plain, open shape between the chairs. · Quick, loose strokes of red, green and white mark the pattern on the chairs. · Her slumped body makes a long diagonal down the chair. · Cassatt wrote that Degas helped with it; he probably painted part of the background."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/summers-day.html","Impressionism","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · c. 1879 · Oil on canvas, 18 × 29 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London · Look at the speed of the strokes, from the zigzags on the dress to the flicks on the water. · Zigzag strokes run down the dress of the woman at the left. · On the water, quick flicks of paint run mostly side to side. · The far shore sits near the top edge, so water fills most of the picture. · The two women are cut off at the left and bottom edges. · Very likely shown in 1880 as The Lake in the Bois de Boulogne, a park in Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere.html","Impressionism","A Bar at the Folies-Bergère","Édouard Manet","manet-folies-bergere","Édouard Manet · 1882 · Oil on canvas, 37 3/4 × 51 1/4 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Look at the mirror behind the barmaid: her reflection and the man's are not where they should be. · The mirror fills the background, so the hall and crowd appear only as reflections. · Her reflection sits far to the right, though she faces us straight on. · A man in a top hat appears in the mirror but not in front of the bar. · Bottles and oranges in front are quick, sure strokes with bright highlights. · Manet made sketches at the hall, then painted the picture in his studio."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/woman-bathing-in-a-shallow-tub.html","Impressionism","Woman Bathing in a Shallow Tub","Edgar Degas","degas-shallow-tub","Edgar Degas · 1885 · Charcoal and pastel on paper, 32 × 22 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Look at the lines that build the body: Degas draws with color, in strokes you can count. · Pastel, sticks of dry color, is laid on in hatched lines across the back. · Pale green and lilac touches on the skin take the place of smooth shading. · The bent body folds into one compact, rounded shape over the tub. · Blue in the tub and ochre on the floor are rubbed in roughly, so the paper's grain shows. · One of a set of nudes Degas showed at the 1886 Impressionist exhibition."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","Impressionism","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-wheat-stacks","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 23 1/2 × 39 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look at the color of the light: this is one of a series, a group of pictures of one subject, painted in different seasons and light. · Low sun turns the edges of the stacks orange and pink. · The sky is pale yellow near the hills and pale green-blue above. · The same pinks, blues and greens are mixed through land, sky and stacks. · Thick strokes cover the whole surface; in the sky they all slant the same way. · Fifteen of the stack paintings hung together in one room at a Paris gallery in May 1891."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-childs-bath.html","Impressionism","The Child's Bath","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-childs-bath","Mary Cassatt · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 26 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look at the flat patterns and clear outlines: Cassatt developed this style after an 1890 show of Japanese prints. · Broad stripes on the woman's dress make a bold, flat pattern. · Firm outlines trace the arms, legs and basin. · Carpet, wallpaper and chest add more patterns, packed edge to edge. · The skin is smooth and solid against the busy patterns around it. · First shown in Cassatt's 1893 exhibition at Durand-Ruel's gallery in Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/rouen-cathedral-west-facade-sunlight.html","Impressionism","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-sunlight","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 39 1/2 × 26 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Look at the surface: the stone front is built from thick, crusted paint. · The paint is rough and lumpy, like the stone it shows. · Up close, the carvings break into patches of white, yellow, blue and pink. · The façade fills almost the whole canvas; blue sky shows only between the towers. · Tiny figures at the lower left show the building's scale. · Monet worked on it in a room facing the cathedral, then finished it at Giverny."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/water-lilies.html","Impressionism","Water Lilies","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-1906","Claude Monet · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/2 × 37 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Look for the horizon: there is none, so the pond's surface fills the whole canvas. · Every edge of the canvas cuts through water or lily pads. · Reflected sky and trees show as soft blue and violet in the water. · Lily pads shrink and flatten toward the top, which suggests distance. · Pads and flowers are thick strokes of green, white and pink over thinner blue paint. · Shown in Paris in 1909, in an exhibition of Monet's water-lily paintings."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints · Overview","What to Look For","","hokusai-red-fuji","Line, flat color and bold design in prints of the floating world · Ukiyo-e, “pictures of the floating world,” were sold as woodblock prints from the 1680s into the 1880s. They were first made in Edo, the city now called Tokyo. Publishers issued them in quantity for the city's townspeople. The most common subjects were courtesans and actors of kabuki, a theater of dance and drama; landscape came later. Each print took four people: a designer, a block carver, a printer and the publisher who issued it for sale. · Katsushika Hokusai · South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji) · about 1830–32"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/the-entrance-to-ageya-machi.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","The Entrance to Ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu","moronobu-entrance-ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu · 1681–84 · Woodblock print; ink on paper, 10 × 15 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hishikawa Moronobu (Japanese, 1618–1694). Everything is drawn in black outline, with no color and no shading. · The lines keep nearly one thickness around faces, robes and roof tiles alike. · Hair, robe patterns, crests and sword scabbards are the solid darks; the street is left blank. · The street is seen from above, and rooftops run across the foreground. · Walls and roofs run on parallel diagonals that never meet, so the ground tilts up like a map. · A samurai and his servant have just come through the gate of the Yoshiwara, the pleasure quarter of Edo."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/on-the-yoshida-highway.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","On the Yoshida Highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed)","sugimura-yoshida-highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed) · about 1685 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 23 1/4 × 11 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Attributed to Sugimura Jihei (Japanese, active about 1681–98). The print itself is black line; the color was brushed on by hand afterward. · Strokes of orange-red and dull green run loosely over collars, sashes and hems and do not fill the outlines. · Most of each robe is pattern printed in black: checks, crests, clouds and leaves. · Four large figures fill the tall sheet in two tiers, with little empty ground. · Beams and a floor edge slope up to the right in the background. · The hand-colored print is a type called tan-e. The labels name the characters, likely from an early play."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/perspective-view-of-the-interior-of-the-nakamura-theater.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Perspective View of the Interior of the Nakamura Theater","Okumura Masanobu","masanobu-nakamura-theater","Okumura Masanobu · 1740 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 15 3/4 × 25 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Okumura Masanobu (Japanese, 1686–1764). Ceiling boards and side galleries slant toward one point behind the stage, and the rows of lanterns shrink with distance. · Heads in the crowd shrink row by row toward the stage. · The two side galleries narrow like the walls of a box, so the hall reads as deep space. · Hand-brushed yellow, red and red-brown are the only colors. · The roofed stage sits small at the center, under three rows of lanterns. · A perspective print of a kabuki theater. The designer was probably the earliest printmaker to use perspective."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/woman-admiring-plum-blossoms-at-night.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","Suzuki Harunobu · about 1766 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 12 3/4 × 8 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Suzuki Harunobu (Japanese, 1725–1770). A solid black night covers about half the sheet. · White blossoms and the lantern stand out sharply against the dark. · The robe is a flat pale-orange shape covered in thin stripes and grey roundels, with no shading. · The orange railing crosses the sheet on a diagonal and is cut off at both sides. · The figure is slight, with a small round face and tiny hands. · A straight-edged band of cloud closes the top. · Full-color printing, with a separate carved block for each color, began in Edo in 1765. The socks and inner robes are embossed: pressed into the paper without ink."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/lovers-walking-in-the-snow-crow-and-heron.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Lovers Walking in the Snow (Crow and Heron)","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-lovers-snow","Suzuki Harunobu · 1764–72 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 11 1/4 × 8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · One figure is a black shape and the other a white one, side by side on a plain ground. · The black robe is a single flat silhouette with almost no inner lines. · The white robe is marked only with fine outline, and a faint diamond pattern is pressed into the surface. · Thin strips of orange and patterned lining show at hems and sleeves. · The yellow umbrella is the one strong color, a wide flat oval above both heads. · Both faces are nearly identical, small and tilted down. · The paired black and white robes explain the print's second title."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/a-boating-party-under-azuma-bridge.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","A Boating Party under Azuma Bridge","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-boating-azuma-bridge","Torii Kiyonaga · about 1785 · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 15 1/4 × 10 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Torii Kiyonaga (Japanese, 1752–1815). The figures are tall and slim, spread across the whole width of the three sheets. · Standing women have small heads on long bodies. · Yellow bridge timbers cross in X shapes behind the figures. · Boats and awnings continue across the joins, though each sheet carries its own signature. · Color is flat and unshaded, and yellow covers the largest area. · The far bank is a low green band high in the picture. · A triptych is one design printed on three sheets. Pleasure boats are tied up in the shade of a bridge on the Sumida River."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/ono-no-komachi-visiting-kiyomizu-temple.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ono no Komachi Visiting Kiyomizu Temple","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-komachi-kiyomizu","Chōbunsai Eishi · about 1788 · Color woodblock print, 15 1/4 × 10 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Chōbunsai Eishi (Japanese, 1756–1829). The colors are few and pale: pink, yellow, olive green and grey. · Black is used in sharp patches: a sash, the hair, roundels on a robe, leaves and twigs. · Long parallel lines run down both robes as stripes and folds. · Hills are soft bands of grey and olive. · Cherry blossom is printed in pale pink with no black line. · Two women and a boy view cherry blossoms at a temple in Kyoto; the boy fastens a slip of paper with a poem to a branch."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/three-beauties-of-the-kansei-period.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Three Beauties of the Kansei Period","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-three-beauties-kansei","Kitagawa Utamaro · about 1791 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 15 1/4 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Kitagawa Utamaro (Japanese, about 1754–1806). Three heads and shoulders fill the sheet, seen close up. · The faces are bare paper, drawn with a few thin lines for nose, eyes and brow. · The hair is solid black, with fine pale lines through the mass to show strands. · The three heads form a triangle, one above and two below. · Robes are flat patches of blue-grey, green and black, with touches of red. · The background is empty except for a title, a signature and seals. · A close-up bust portrait is called an ōkubi-e, a “big-head picture.”"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/otani-oniji-iii-as-yakko-edobei.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-otani-oniji","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 15 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Tōshūsai Sharaku (Japanese, active 1794–95). Face and hands are pushed toward caricature and set against a dark, flat ground. · Eyebrows, eyes and mouth are a few heavy black strokes, pulled into a scowl. · The hands are small and stiff, with fingers spread like claws, out of scale with the head. · Face, neck and hands are left pale and stand out from the black robe. · Bright red is kept to the eye rims and one strip of inner robe. · A kabuki actor plays a ruthless manservant. The mica in the print is a mineral powder."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 10 × 15 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Katsushika Hokusai (Japanese, 1760–1849). A huge near wave is set against a tiny far mountain. · Mount Fuji is a small white triangle low in the hollow of the wave. · The foam breaks into rows of small hooked fingers, each with a dark blue outline. · Three blues, dark, middle and pale, stripe the body of the wave. · The sky is pale above, with one faint cloud shape, and darkens to grey at the horizon. · The curve of the wave nearly closes a circle around the empty center. · From the series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji. The artist was known for landscapes printed with indigo and Prussian blue, an imported pigment."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/south-wind-clear-sky-red-fuji.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-red-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 9 1/2 × 14 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The picture is three flat shapes: a red mountain, a blue sky and a green base. · The red of the mountain fades to brown at the peak and to green at the foot, with no line between the colors. · The sky is darkest blue along the top edge and pales downward. · Clouds are flat white strips in even rows. · The forest is hundreds of small dark dabs, with no trunks or outlines. · The grain of the wooden block shows as faint lines across the red. · Color graded in this way is called bokashi: the printer wipes the color by hand."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/shichirigahama-in-sagami-province.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Shichirigahama in Sagami Province","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-shichirigahama","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 10 × 15 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Nearly the whole print is in shades of one color, blue. · Sea, sky, trees and hills share the same blue, from a pale wash to deep navy. · Some trees and the near hill turn blue-green, the only shift in color. · A dark band runs along the horizon and fades upward into blank paper. · Mount Fuji and the clouds are left white. · Waves along the shore are rows of small repeated curls. · A print in which Prussian blue dominates is called aizuri, “blue printing.”"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/evening-snow-at-kanbara.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Evening Snow at Kanbara","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Utagawa Hiroshige · about 1833–34 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 8 3/4 × 13 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Utagawa Hiroshige (Japanese, 1797–1858). The picture is nearly all grey and white, with color only on three small figures. · Snow is bare paper; roofs, hills and road are shaped by the grey around them. · The sky is grey and dotted with white flakes. · Grey shading fades softly on the slopes, without hatched lines. · The road climbs on a diagonal from the lower left. · A station on the Tōkaidō, the road between Edo and Kyoto. The snow is imagined: records indicate the artist passed through in summer."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/driving-rain-at-shono.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Driving Rain at Shōno","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-shono-rain","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1833 · Color woodblock print, 10 × 15 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Everything slants: the rain one way, the hillside path the other. · Rain is printed as fine straight lines slanting across the whole sheet. · The path cuts the picture on the opposite diagonal, from the left edge to the lower right. · Bamboo behind is rows of grey silhouette with no outlines, each row darkest at the crest. · The figures are hunched wedges under hats, capes and an umbrella. · The strongest colors are the blue of the path and the yellow of straw capes and an umbrella. · Two groups of travelers pass on a slope at another Tōkaidō station. Three separate blocks printed the greys of the bamboo."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/ghosts-of-the-taira-at-daimotsu-bay.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1849–52 · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 14 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Utagawa Kuniyoshi (Japanese, 1797–1861). Sharp, bright shapes in front are set against soft dark silhouettes behind. · The ghosts are flat shadows in grey and brown, with no outline and no detail. · Ship, sail and crew are drawn in crisp line and full color. · Foam along the hull is white and clawed; the great wave beside it is smooth bands of blue. · The design spreads over the three sheets: ship at left, sail at center, wave at right. · Small yellow and red labels are set among the figures on deck. · The ghosts of the Taira clan rise against a ship of the Minamoto, who defeated them in a sea battle in 1185."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/plum-garden-at-kameido.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Plum Garden at Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kameido-plum","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 14 1/4 × 9 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · A dark tree trunk is pushed right up against the viewer and cropped by the edges of the sheet. · Trunk and branches are flat dark shapes that cross the whole picture. · The sky is red and fades to white at the horizon; the ground is a flat green. · Between the branches, the far trees and visitors are tiny. · Thin straight shoots rise from the branches to the top edge. · Blossoms are white disks set on the red and the green. · From One Hundred Famous Views of Edo. Vincent van Gogh painted a copy of the design in 1887."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/fukagawa-susaki-and-jumantsubo.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Fukagawa Susaki and Jūmantsubo","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-fukagawa-susaki","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 13 × 8 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · The view is from high in the air, with an eagle hanging across the top of the sheet. · The eagle is cut off by the top edge, with wings spread across the full width. · The land below is a pattern of small marks for trees and huts. · The water darkens to deep blue at the bottom of the sheet. · Snow on the ground is unprinted, and white specks in the sky are falling flakes. · Feathers are built from rows of small scalloped lines in grey and brown. · Edo Bay under snow. Viewers at the time could only imagine such a view from the air."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/moon-over-daimotsu-bay-benkei.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Moon over Daimotsu Bay: Benkei","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi","yoshitoshi-benkei-moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi · 1886 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 13 3/4 × 9 3/4 in. Minneapolis Institute of Art · Tsukioka Yoshitoshi (Japanese, 1839–1892). A tall black wave curls up the left side, and its foam reaches across the moon. · Black and grey cover almost the whole sheet. · Foam is drawn in thin lines that fray into wisps. · Cloud and water are layered in several greys, from pale to near black. · The moon is a pale disk half hidden by cloud and foam, the brightest shape in the sky. · Bright red marks one cord at the figure's chest and the seal at lower left. · The warrior-monk Benkei prays in a storm so that slain enemies will not rise from the water. From the series One Hundred Aspects of the Moon."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme.html","Japonisme · Overview","What to Look For","","vangogh-flowering-plum-orchard","Japanese prints, props and design in Western art · Japonisme is the French name for a passion for the art and design of Japan that took hold in France and spread through Europe and North America after Japan resumed trade with the West in the 1850s. The critic Philippe Burty is generally credited with the word, in the early 1870s. Some artists painted fans, kimono and prints as studio props; others borrowed what the woodblock prints did with form: flat, bright color, firm outlines, long or tall formats, views from above, figures cut by the edge and wide empty areas. · Vincent van Gogh · Flowering Plum Orchard (after Hiroshige) · 1887"],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/caprice-in-purple-and-gold-the-golden-screen.html","Japonisme","Caprice in Purple and Gold: The Golden Screen","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-golden-screen","James McNeill Whistler · 1864 · Oil on wood panel, 19 3/4 × 27 in. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian, Washington · James McNeill Whistler (1834–1903). A gold folding screen fills the back of the room behind a model in purple and red robes. · The woman sits low on the floor, and her robes spread out toward the lower right. · Strong red lining stands out between the purple kimono and the white under-robe. · Prints lie scattered on the floor at the right while she holds another up to read. · Buildings and small figures on the screen are brushed loosely in green and grey over the gold. · The model, Joanna Hiffernan, poses as a Japanese courtesan, studying a print from Hiroshige's series Views of the Sixty-odd Provinces."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/variations-in-flesh-colour-and-green-the-balcony.html","Japonisme","Variations in Flesh Colour and Green: The Balcony","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-balcony","James McNeill Whistler · 1864–70 · Oil on wood panel, 24 1/4 × 19 1/4 in. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian, Washington · The thin uprights of an iron railing run behind four women in kimono on a balcony over the misty Thames, with factory chimneys beyond. · A flat band of turquoise floor fills the lower third, with flowering branches along the bottom edge. · The far shore is a misty strip of greys, with chimneys and a dark peaked heap. · A green blind and an upright post frame the top and right edges. · A pale panel at lower left holds a small butterfly. · The museum compares the heap beside the chimneys to Mount Fuji in prints by Hiroshige and Hokusai. From this picture onward, Whistler used a butterfly in place of a signature."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/soup-plate-rousseau-service.html","Japonisme","Soup Plate, Rousseau Service","Félix Bracquemond","bracquemond-rousseau-plate","Félix Bracquemond · 1866–75 · Creamware, diameter 9 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Félix Bracquemond (French, 1833–1914). A turkey sits just off-center and a moth, an insect and a flowering sprig sit toward the rim, all drawn in dark outline on the white plate. · Wide white space surrounds every motif. · Color comes in a few touches: orange on the head, pink on the breast, pale green leaves. · Feathers are rows of small scalloped marks. · A ragged band of blue-violet edges the rim. · Bracquemond owned Hokusai's Manga, a many-volume book of sketches, earlier than most Europeans, and his designs for the service draw on it."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/boating.html","Japonisme","Boating","Édouard Manet","manet-boating","Édouard Manet · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 38 1/4 × 51 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Édouard Manet (French, 1832–1883). The side of the boat and the mast cut across the canvas in strong diagonals. · Turquoise water rises to the top edge as a flat backdrop. · The man's white clothes make a large light shape at the center. · The woman is cut off by the bottom edge, the boat by the bottom and right edges. · Her dress is quick blue and white strokes. · The museum says Manet used the broad planes of color and the diagonals of Japanese prints here, along with the lighter palette of the Impressionists. Exhibited at the 1879 Salon."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/la-japonaise-camille-monet-in-japanese-costume.html","Japonisme","La Japonaise (Camille Monet in Japanese Costume)","Claude Monet","monet-la-japonaise","Claude Monet · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 91 1/4 × 56 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Claude Monet (French, 1840–1926). A red kimono embroidered in gold and blue fills the center of a canvas more than seven feet tall. · Round Japanese fans are scattered over the grey-green wall and the floor. · The woman turns back over her shoulder and raises an open folding fan. · An embroidered warrior drawing a sword stands out in blue and gold on the robe. · The kimono's train spreads in a wide curve across the matting. · The museum reads the picture as a playful jab at the Paris taste for all things Japanese; Camille Monet wears a blond wig that marks her as Western. Shown at the second Impressionist exhibition, 1876."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/dancers-in-the-rehearsal-room-with-a-double-bass.html","Japonisme","Dancers in the Rehearsal Room with a Double Bass","Edgar Degas","degas-rehearsal-double-bass","Edgar Degas · about 1882–85 · Oil on canvas, 15 1/2 × 35 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Edgar Degas (French, 1834–1917). The long, narrow canvas sets the dancers at the right and gives much of the left to wall and floor. · A double bass lies on the floor at lower left. · A dancer bends low over her slipper, her skirt a pale burst of white and yellow. · The floor is seen from above and tilts up toward the windows. · Dancers in the far room are loose strokes of blue, red and white. · Colta Ives of the Met says Degas avoided Japanese props and took over principles instead: long formats, off-center designs, high viewpoints and emptied spaces."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/flowering-plum-orchard-after-hiroshige.html","Japonisme","Flowering Plum Orchard (after Hiroshige)","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-flowering-plum-orchard","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 22 × 18 1/2 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Vincent van Gogh (1853–1890). Strong red, green and orange fill a painted copy of a Japanese print of plum trees in bloom. · A dark trunk crosses the picture on a diagonal in front of a garden of white blossom. · The sky shades from red through pink to pale yellow. · Two orange borders hold large characters painted in dark green. · The trunk is reddish brown with blue-black shadow. · The first of three paintings he made after prints in his own collection, here Hiroshige's Plum Garden in Kameido. He followed the design closely, strengthened the color and added the borders."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/bridge-in-the-rain-after-hiroshige.html","Japonisme","Bridge in the Rain (after Hiroshige)","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-bridge-in-the-rain","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 21 1/4 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Rain falls in long dark strokes over a yellow bridge seen from above. · The bridge climbs on a diagonal from the left edge toward the upper right. · Small figures under hats and cloaks hurry across in both directions. · The river is green, broken by short blue and white dashes. · A red and green border full of Japanese characters frames the scene. · Based on a print by Hiroshige. Van Gogh used a standard canvas and filled the extra space with characters copied from other prints."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/volpini-suite-laundresses-les-laveuses.html","Japonisme","Volpini Suite: Laundresses (Les Laveuses)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-laundresses","Paul Gauguin · 1889 · Zincograph on yellow paper, image 8 1/4 × 10 1/4 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Paul Gauguin (French, 1848–1903). Bright yellow paper carries the black lines and dark masses of the print. · A woman bends over the water, seen from behind and above. · Froth on the water is drawn as wavy, looping lines. · A cow at the lower left is cut off by the border. · Dark areas are dense crayon tone set flat against the yellow. · The museum points to the strange viewpoint, the stylized foam and the cropped cow as signs of Japanese woodblock prints. One of his first prints, drawn on zinc plates."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/almond-blossom.html","Japonisme","Almond Blossom","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-almond-blossom","Vincent van Gogh · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 36 1/2 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Almond branches in bloom spread across the canvas, seen close and from below, with the edges cutting them off. · The blue background is flat, with visible brushwork but little depth. · Branches are drawn with dark outlines. · Blossoms are thick dabs of white and pale pink. · The trunk enters from the bottom edge, and the frame crops the rest of the tree. · The museum says the choice of subject, the strong outlines and the tree's position in the picture are borrowed from Japanese printmaking. A gift for his brother Theo on the birth of Theo's son."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/the-letter.html","Japonisme","The Letter","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-letter","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Color aquatint and drypoint, plate 13 3/4 × 8 1/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Mary Cassatt (American, 1844–1926). Pattern covers the woman's blue dress and the wallpaper behind her. · Color sits in flat areas of deep blue, brown and pale pink. · The desk tilts up steeply, its blue top a sharp triangle. · Fine dark lines outline the face and hands. · A tall brown bureau closes the right side. · One of ten color prints she made after seeing an exhibition of Japanese prints in Paris in spring 1890."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/woman-bathing.html","Japonisme","Woman Bathing","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-woman-bathing","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Color aquatint and drypoint, plate 14 1/2 × 10 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · The room is seen from above, so the floor tilts up behind the bending woman. · Her bare back is a smooth curve drawn in a fine line. · Pink, green and white stripes run down her gown. · Leaf patterns spread over the blue carpet and the jug. · Part of her head is reflected in the mirror. · The museum compares the color, pattern and shapes to choices made by Japanese printmakers such as Hiroshige."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/moulin-rouge-la-goulue.html","Japonisme","Moulin Rouge: La Goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-la-goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1891 · Color lithograph, three sheets joined, 74 3/4 × 46 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec (French, 1864–1901). Bold red and black lettering fills the top and bottom of a poster more than six feet tall. · A row of black silhouettes in top hats runs behind the dancer. · Her white petticoats are a flat light shape with few lines. · The bottom edge crops a grey figure in the foreground. · Yellow lamps are simple flat half-circles. · The Met's Colta Ives credits Japanese prints of Kabuki actors for the strong color, outlines and facial expressions in his posters."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/the-protest.html","Japonisme","The Protest","Félix Vallotton","vallotton-protest","Félix Vallotton · 1893 · Woodcut, image 8 × 12 1/2 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Félix Vallotton (Swiss French, 1865–1925). Solid black figures race across white paper, seen from above. · The street tilts up steeply toward the buildings at the top left. · The empty lower-right corner takes up about a third of the sheet. · Checks and dots break up a few of the black coats. · The figures are flat shapes with almost no modeling. · The museum links the flat space, tipped-up view and pattern to Japanese color woodcuts. Police are breaking up a political demonstration."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/nursemaids-promenade-frieze-of-carriages.html","Japonisme","Nursemaids' Promenade, Frieze of Carriages","Pierre Bonnard","bonnard-nursemaids-promenade","Pierre Bonnard · 1895 · Color lithographs, four-panel screen, each panel 54 1/4 × 18 3/4 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Pierre Bonnard (French, 1867–1947). Four tall panels, made to stand as a folding screen, carry one scene from left to right. · A row of carriages runs along the top like a frieze. · Much of each panel is bare paper. · The nanny's cape is a flat brown shape with few inner lines. · A hoop drawn as a thin circle floats in the second panel. · The museum says Bonnard copied the flat blocks of color in the Japanese color woodcuts he collected."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/boats-at-rest.html","Japonisme","Boats at Rest","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-boats-at-rest","Arthur Wesley Dow · about 1895 · Oil on canvas, 26 × 36 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Arthur Wesley Dow (American, 1857–1922). Rowboats on a riverbank are seen from above, with a shed cut off at the left edge. · The river curves up toward a high horizon near the top. · Blue and green boats stand against red-brown ground. · Trees and buildings are reflected as flat shapes in the water. · Tall grass in the foreground is brushed in long, loose strokes. · The museum points to cropping, a raised viewpoint and flattened space taken from Japanese prints. Through his books and teaching, Dow's ideas on composition shaped many artists and designers."],[6,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/water-lily-pond.html","Japonisme","Water Lily Pond","Claude Monet","monet-water-lily-pond","Claude Monet · 1900 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/2 × 39 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · The curve of a footbridge in the Japanese style arches across the upper half of the canvas. · Lily pads fill the lower half in horizontal dabs of green, pink and white. · Reflections of willows run down the water in vertical strokes. · The bridge runs off the right edge. · Thick foliage closes the top, with almost no sky. · In 1893 Monet began making a pond at Giverny and spanned it with a wooden footbridge in the Japanese manner. The canvas is one of eighteen similar versions from 1899–1900."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism.html","Neoclassicism · Overview","What to Look For","","david-horatii","Rome, Paris and London, from the 1760s to the first years of Napoleon · Neoclassicism is the return to the clear lines, calm order and stern subjects of ancient Greece and Rome, from the painters working in Rome in the 1760s to David's Paris around the Revolution. · Jacques-Louis David · The Oath of the Horatii · 1784–85"],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-lucretia.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Lucretia","Gavin Hamilton","hamilton-lucretia","Gavin Hamilton · 1763–67 · Oil on canvas, 84 × 104 in. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Gavin Hamilton (Scottish, 1723–1798). The figures stand in a row across the front like a carved relief, with big, readable gestures. · A dark curtain closes off the space behind the figures, so nothing opens up beyond them. · The raised dagger sits just right of centre, where the outstretched arm meets the old man's grip, and the arms on both sides lead toward it. · The colours come in broad blocks: a red cloak, a blue mantle, yellow and violet drapery, against a dark ground. · The outlines are firm and the flesh is evenly modelled, with no loose touch. · Roman men swearing revenge over the body of Lucretia, painted in Rome about twenty years before David's Horatii."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/portrait-of-a-young-man.html","Neoclassicism","Portrait of a Young Man","Pompeo Batoni","batoni-young-man","Pompeo Batoni · c. 1760–65 · Oil on canvas, 97 1/4 × 69 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Pompeo Batoni (Italian, 1708–1787). The props tell the story: an antique statue and relief beside the sitter. · An antique statue on a plinth, a marble relief, an armillary sphere and a pile of books fill the left side, and the sitter's open hand points to them. · The red suit with gold embroidery is the strongest colour, set at the centre between the gilt table and the green chair. · A window at the right opens on blue sky and a green landscape with distant hills. · The surfaces are rendered with the same even polish, from the velvet to the marble floor. · A Grand Tour portrait: a young visitor to Rome shown with a statue of Minerva, a relief of Antinous, a sphere and books."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-general-wolfe.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of General Wolfe","Benjamin West","west-death-of-wolfe","Benjamin West · 1770 · Oil on canvas, 60 × 84 1/2 in. National Gallery of Canada, Ottawa · Benjamin West (American, 1738–1820). The pose is that of a mourning group around a fallen hero, but the mourners wear the dress of 1759, not togas. · The dying general lies in a lit triangle at the centre, held by officers who lean in from both sides. · A dark storm cloud and the raised flag frame the group from above. · Red coats repeat across the canvas, set off by the green coat at the left and the blue coat beside the general. · The battle is pushed back into a smoky strip on the horizon, so the foreground reads as a mourning scene. · A general dying at Quebec in 1759."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/johann-joachim-winckelmann.html","Neoclassicism","Johann Joachim Winckelmann","Anton Raphael Mengs","mengs-winckelmann","Anton Raphael Mengs · c. 1777 · Oil on canvas, 25 × 19 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Anton Raphael Mengs (German, 1728–1779). The finish is smooth and brushless, on a plain, sober ground. · The face and the open white shirt catch light from the left against a flat olive-brown background. · The colour is a narrow range of browns, white and the pale yellow of the book, with one note of green cloth at the lower left. · The skin is blended to a porcelain smoothness, with no stroke showing; the brown robe is painted more broadly. · The picture holds only the head, the hand and the book: no wig, no setting, no ornament. · The scholar whose history of ancient art shaped the movement, shown holding a copy of Homer's Iliad."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/voltaire.html","Neoclassicism","Voltaire","Jean-Antoine Houdon","houdon-voltaire","Jean-Antoine Houdon · 1778 · Marble, 20 3/4 × 18 × 13 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Jean-Antoine Houdon (French, 1741–1828). A lifelike bust: an old, lined, smiling face in the format of an ancient portrait. · The wig is a mass of deep-drilled curls that catch dark shadows, against the smooth skin of the face. · The thin lips pull up into a slight smile and the eyes are cut so a bright spot of marble catches the light. · The coat and waistcoat fall in broad, plain planes, which keeps attention on the head. · The bust is cut off below the chest and set on a round turned socle, the shape of a Roman portrait bust. · The philosopher in his last year, in his own coat and wig."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/cenotaph-for-newton.html","Neoclassicism","Cenotaph for Newton","Étienne-Louis Boullée","boullee-newton","Étienne-Louis Boullée · 1784 · Ink and wash, sheet 17 1/4 × 26 in. Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris · Étienne-Louis Boullée (French, 1728–1799). Geometry as architecture: one pure sphere on a ring of plain bases. · A single sphere fills the upper half of the sheet, set on two stepped circular bases. · Rings of cypress trees run around each base, drawn as even rows of small dark spikes. · Grey wash shades the sphere from light at the lower left to dark at the top, so the flat drawing reads as a solid globe. · The people on the steps are specks, which makes the building seem immense. · The surfaces are bare: no columns, no windows, only the plain curves of cylinder and sphere. · An unbuilt monument to Isaac Newton, signed by the architect."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/cornelia-mother-of-the-gracchi-pointing-to-her-children-as-her-treasures.html","Neoclassicism","Cornelia, Mother of the Gracchi, Pointing to Her Children as Her Treasures","Angelica Kauffman","kauffman-cornelia","Angelica Kauffman · c. 1785 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 50 in. Virginia Museum of Fine Arts, Richmond · Angelica Kauffman (Swiss, 1741–1807). The colour is calm and even, and the figures stand before plain columns. · Cornelia stands in white at the centre, the brightest shape, between her sons at the left and the seated visitor at the right. · The colours are white, cream, two clear reds and ochre, each kept to its own flat area, with one blue cloth on the stool. · Two heavy columns and a plain wall stand behind the figures, with open sky between them. · The gestures are slow and clear: Cornelia's open hand toward the boys, the visitor's hand holding up a chain of jewels. · The figures are evenly lit, with soft edges and no deep shadow. · A Roman mother and a visitor who has come to show off her jewellery."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-oath-of-the-horatii.html","Neoclassicism","The Oath of the Horatii","Jacques-Louis David","david-horatii","Jacques-Louis David · 1784–85 · Oil on canvas, 130 × 167 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Jacques-Louis David (French, 1748–1825). Hard outlines and straight limbs, in a space no deeper than a stage. · Three arches behind the figures divide the scene into three groups: the sons, the father with the swords, the women. · The men are built from straight lines and sharp angles, legs braced and arms thrust out; the women collapse in soft curves. · The floor runs in a flat grid to a plain grey wall, which closes the space a few steps behind the figures. · Light falls from the left onto the swords and the father's raised hand, and the three blades meet at the centre. · Three Roman brothers swear to fight for their city; shown at the Paris Salon of 1785."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/self-portrait-with-two-pupils.html","Neoclassicism","Self-Portrait with Two Pupils","Adélaïde Labille-Guiard","labille-guiard-pupils","Adélaïde Labille-Guiard · 1785 · Oil on canvas, 83 × 59 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Adélaïde Labille-Guiard (French, 1749–1803). The rendering is the point: satin, lace, straw and skin, all at full sharpness. · The silver-blue silk dress is the largest and brightest shape, its highlights laid on in crisp strokes. · The lace at the neckline, the straw hat and the feathers are each painted with their own texture. · The two pupils lean together at the right in grey and olive-brown, a muted vertical group against a dark wall. · The easel cuts in from the left, seen from behind, so the painter's own canvas is hidden. · A plaster bust and a statue stand in the shadow behind, pale against the dark. · A painter at her easel with her two students, shown at the Salon of 1785."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-socrates.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Socrates","Jacques-Louis David","david-socrates","Jacques-Louis David · 1787 · Oil on canvas, 51 × 77 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Line and gesture carry the picture: one raised finger, one hand reaching for the cup. · Socrates sits upright just right of centre, the most brightly lit figure, with one arm raised and the other stretched out to the side. · The grieving followers on both sides bend and turn away, and their curved backs frame the straight body in the middle. · The stone wall runs flat across the picture, broken only by an arched passage at the left. · Every fold and limb has a clean edge; the colours are flat blocks of red, white, ochre, blue and grey. · The philosopher drinks the poison rather than renounce his teaching; the old man at the foot of the bed is Plato."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/antoine-laurent-lavoisier-and-marie-anne-lavoisier.html","Neoclassicism","Antoine Laurent Lavoisier and Marie Anne Lavoisier","Jacques-Louis David","david-lavoisier","Jacques-Louis David · 1788 · Oil on canvas, 102 1/4 × 76 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Clarity: every glass instrument is described exactly, down to the reflections. · The glass vessels on the table and the globe on the floor are painted with sharp highlights and exact reflections, more precise than anything else on the canvas. · The white dress forms one large pale shape at the left, balanced by the red velvet tablecloth at the right. · Her standing figure leans down and his seated figure looks up, so the two make one connected diagonal. · The background is a grey wall with flat pilasters, plain and unornamented. · A chemist and his wife at a table of laboratory glassware."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/psyche-revived-by-cupids-kiss.html","Neoclassicism","Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss","Antonio Canova","canova-psyche","Antonio Canova · 1787–93 · Marble and metal, 61 × 66 1/4 × 39 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Antonio Canova (Italian, 1757–1822). Polished marble made to look like soft skin, in two crossing diagonals. · Cupid's legs and wings make one long diagonal from the lower left to the upper right; Psyche's body and raised arms make the other, and the two faces sit where they cross. · The arms form a ring around the two heads, so the eye circles back to the centre. · The hair and the drapery are cut in finer, rougher detail than the bodies. · Thin folds of cloth slip from Psyche's hips and spill over the rock below. · Cupid waking Psyche with a kiss, from the ancient tale of the two lovers."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-sleep-of-endymion.html","Neoclassicism","The Sleep of Endymion","Anne-Louis Girodet","girodet-endymion","Anne-Louis Girodet · 1791 · Oil on canvas, 78 × 102 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Anne-Louis Girodet (French, 1767–1824). A single moonbeam falls on an idealised body in deep shadow. · A shaft of pale light enters from the upper left through the leaves and falls diagonally across the sleeping figure. · The body is smooth and soft-edged, lit from above, with the hard outline of David's figures softened away. · Apart from one muted rose cloth and the spotted leopard skin, the colour is held to silvery flesh, browns and dark greens, so the picture reads as night. · The leaves at the top are painted in sharp detail against the glow of the sky. · Endymion asleep on a leopard skin while the boy Zephyr parts the branches; painted in Rome and shown at the Salon of 1793."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-marat.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Marat","Jacques-Louis David","david-marat","Jacques-Louis David · 1793 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 50 1/2 in. Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels · Empty space: the upper half of the canvas is left as bare, brushed brown wall. · All the figures and objects sit in a band across the bottom; the wall above carries nothing at all. · Light from the upper left picks out the head, the shoulder and the hanging arm against the dark. · The wooden crate at the front is flat and plain, lettered with a dedication like an inscribed tomb slab. · The colours are few: white sheet, green cloth, yellow wood and the pale body. · The arm hangs straight down and the head rests on the shoulder, like a figure from a Deposition. · A murdered revolutionary journalist; the crate carries the painter's dedication, dated to year two of the Republic."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-intervention-of-the-sabine-women.html","Neoclassicism","The Intervention of the Sabine Women","Jacques-Louis David","david-sabines","Jacques-Louis David · 1799 · Oil on canvas, 151 1/2 × 205 1/2 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Nude warriors with the outlines of statues, held mid-action. · The two main warriors are nude, each outlined cleanly against the crowd, with their bodies modelled as smoothly as marble. · The woman in white stands between the two warriors with arms flung wide, a shield raised at her on either side. · The front figures are lit evenly and sharply; the fortress behind softens in haze and smoke. · A forest of spears runs across the middle of the canvas in thin straight lines. · Women throw themselves between their Roman husbands and their Sabine fathers to end a battle."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/madame-recamier.html","Neoclassicism","Madame Récamier","Jacques-Louis David","david-recamier","Jacques-Louis David · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 68 1/2 × 96 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Sparseness: a bare room, one piece of furniture and a great deal of empty wall. · The low daybed and the reclining figure run almost the full width of the canvas. · The white dress is the lightest shape, set off by the yellow daybed and the grey-brown wall. · The background is loosely brushed and thin, while the face and the dress are carried further. · The furniture is plain and antique in shape: a lamp on a tall stand, a daybed with scrolled ends, a footstool. · Juliette Récamier in a white dress; the painter left the picture a sketch."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/napoleon-crossing-the-alps.html","Neoclassicism","Napoleon Crossing the Alps","Jacques-Louis David","david-napoleon-alps","Jacques-Louis David · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 102 × 87 in. Musée national du château de Malmaison, Rueil-Malmaison · One strong diagonal: a rearing horse against the slope. · The horse and rider form a single diagonal from the hind legs at the lower right to the horse's head and the flying cloak at the upper left. · The yellow cloak and the horse's mane and tail blow in the same direction, so the whole figure seems pushed by wind. · The soldiers and cannon on the slope behind are tiny, so the rider fills most of the canvas. · The surface is glassy and even, with the uniform, bridle and saddle picked out in sharp detail. · The First Consul crossing the Alps in 1800; the rocks below carry the names of Bonaparte, Hannibal and Charlemagne."],[7,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/perseus-with-the-head-of-medusa.html","Neoclassicism","Perseus with the Head of Medusa","Antonio Canova","canova-perseus","Antonio Canova · 1804–6 · Marble, 95 1/2 × 75 1/2 × 40 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Contrapposto, the stance of the ancient statues, with every limb kept clear of the body. · The figure stands frontal with the weight on the right leg, the left leg trailing back, and the hips and shoulders tilted against each other. · The arm holding the head rises above the shoulder, a strong line thrust out from the upright body. · The cloak over the arm is cut in deep folds, and the hair and the winged cap in fine detail, against the plain surface of the body. · The cloak hangs behind the figure rather than across it, so the silhouette stays open. · A modern marble modelled on the Apollo Belvedere, made after Napoleon's troops took the ancient statue to Paris."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Oil vs Acrylic","","rand-1841-tube-patent","For over five centuries oil has been the paint most painters chose, and John Rand's metal tube of 1841 made it easy to carry. Acrylic came out of the plastics industry, first as a solvent paint called Magna, then in water; by 1965 most makers of artists' paint sold a water-based acrylic."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/two-binders.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Two Binders","","ostade-painter-studio","In oil paint the pigment is carried by a plant oil that hardens on contact with air; in acrylic it is mixed with a cloud of tiny plastic droplets suspended in water. · Behind the easel, two young assistants bend over a small table under the stairs, grinding colors for the painter. · The nearer assistant works at a small pot with both hands; the one behind him bends over the same table with a tool raised. · Pieter Bruegel the Elder · The Harvesters · 1565 · The same oil serves for washes so thin that the drawing underneath still shows and for strokes thick enough to hide it. · Water thins acrylic and cleans its brushes; oil's solvents call for ventilation, and oily rags, which can heat up and ignite by themselves, belong in water in a closed can or a metal fireproof bin."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/two-binders.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","thumb/bruegel-harvesters","1565 · The same oil serves for washes so thin that the drawing underneath still shows and for strokes thick enough to hide it. · Two Binders"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/oil-cures.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Oil Cures","","oa-oil-cures","Oil paint does not dry by evaporating; it cures, taking up oxygen until its molecules join into a single solid mesh. · Every arrow of oxygen meets the top surface; the paint below has to wait for oxygen to work its way down to it. · A deeper amber band runs along the top of the layer, where the oxygen has already acted; below it the paint is drawn pale all the way down to the ground. · Albert Pinkham Ryder · Moonlight Marine · 1870–90 · Ryder laid on thick paint layer after layer; the Met reports that the outlines of clouds, sails and hull have shifted since he finished. · Painters work \"fat over lean\", the oilier paint on top, so each layer stays more flexible than the one beneath; a top coat that sets over still-soft paint can wrinkle and crack."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/oil-cures.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Moonlight Marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder","thumb/ryder-moonlight-marine","1870–90 · Ryder laid on thick paint layer after layer; the Met reports that the outlines of clouds, sails and hull have shifted since he finished. · Oil Cures"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/acrylic-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Acrylic Dries","","oa-acrylic-dries","Acrylic dries by losing water: as it leaves, the plastic droplets crowd together and fuse into a film. · In the middle panel the water line has dropped and the droplets are pressed against one another. · In the last panel they have become one film, thinner than the stack of droplets it came from. · David Nicholson · Early, Still · 2025 · Each flat block set quickly enough for the next to go straight over it, and no color underneath has smudged into the layer above it. · For about a week after it feels dry, sometimes longer, water can still soften acrylic; dried in the cold, the droplets merge poorly and leave a porous or powdery film."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/acrylic-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Early, Still","David Nicholson","thumb/dn-early-still","2025 · Each flat block set quickly enough for the next to go straight over it, and no color underneath has smudged into the layer above it. · Acrylic Dries"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/open-time.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Open Time","","vermeer-lady-writing-detail","Open time is how long paint stays workable on the canvas: acrylic is dry to the touch in minutes to a few hours, oil in about two days. · The fingers and the folds of the blue cloth have soft edges, with no crisp outline where one shape meets the next. · Researchers at the National Gallery of Art trace that blur to strokes laid into paint that was still wet, which oil's long open time allows. · Claude Monet · Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer) · 1890–91 · Monet used both timings: the outline of the bigger stack was worked wet into wet, and in the sky more color went on once earlier strokes had dried. · Because oil cures so slowly, painters are advised to wait six months or more before varnishing an oil painting."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/open-time.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer","1890–91 · Monet used both timings: the outline of the bigger stack was worked wet into wet, and in the sky more color went on once earlier strokes had dried. · Open Time"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/color-shift-as-it-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Color Shift as It Dries","","oa-color-shift","Acrylic goes on with a milky cast; as it dries the binder turns clear, and colors often dry slightly darker. · In the left square pale rings are scattered among the red grains, and the whole square looks lighter. · In the right square the rings are gone and the same red reads deeper. · David Nicholson · Umbrella, Reverie · 2024 · The painter recalls these colors deepening as the acrylic dried. · Dry acrylic can also look flatter than it did wet; a varnish brings that depth back."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/color-shift-as-it-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Umbrella, Reverie","David Nicholson","thumb/dn-umbrella-reverie","2024 · The painter recalls these colors deepening as the acrylic dried. · Color Shift as It Dries"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/blending.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Blending","","ginevra-blending-detail","Blending means working two colors into each other while both are still wet, so the change from one to the other is gradual. · The brown trees thin into the pale sky in soft, broken patches, without a firm outline. · The National Gallery of Art notes that Leonardo used his hands to blend where the sky meets the juniper; his fingerprints are still in the paint. · Pietro Rotari · A Girl with a Flower in Her Hair · 1760–62 · The National Gallery of Art reports delicate blended brushstrokes in her face, with the flower and her features finished last. · To keep acrylic workable, painters mist the palette or add a little retarder; overdoing the retarder gives a soft paint film that can stay tacky."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/blending.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","A Girl with a Flower in Her Hair","Pietro Rotari","thumb/rotari-girl-flower","1760–62 · The National Gallery of Art reports delicate blended brushstrokes in her face, with the flower and her features finished last. · Blending"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/impasto.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Impasto","","van-gogh-green-wheat-detail","Impasto is paint applied so thickly that it rises off the surface and holds the shape of each brush or knife stroke. · Where the lavender swirls cross the white, they sit on top as raised bands instead of mixing into it. · Fine grooves left by the bristles run through each swirl, and the paint folds over into small lips where the brush lifted away. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Lucretia · 1664 · The National Gallery of Art finds extensive palette-knife work in her left cuff and in the lower part of her dress. · Some oil colors are stiff enough from the tube to hold peaks on their own; acrylic texture is often built with gels or pastes, thickened mediums with fillers such as calcite or silica."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/impasto.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Lucretia","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-lucretia","1664 · The National Gallery of Art finds extensive palette-knife work in her left cuff and in the lower part of her dress. · Impasto"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/painting-over-dry-paint.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Painting Over Dry Paint","","oa-painting-over","Once a layer is dry it can be changed or covered completely, and changes hidden this way are called pentimenti. · The two red shapes underneath are complete forms of their own, hidden by a single later layer. · The dashed lines turn solid where they enter the top layer, and their arrowheads stop just short of the red shapes. · Titian · Venus with a Mirror · about 1555 · X-rays show a double portrait of a man and a woman underneath, turned on its side; Titian kept the man's jacket and turned it into the red velvet wrap over her hip. · Oil paint may be laid over acrylic, but acrylic should not be put over oil."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/painting-over-dry-paint.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Venus with a Mirror","Titian","thumb/titian-venus-with-a-mirror","about 1555 · X-rays show a double portrait of a man and a woman underneath, turned on its side; Titian kept the man's jacket and turned it into the red velvet wrap over her hip. · Painting Over Dry Paint"],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/how-oil-ages.html","Oil vs Acrylic","How Oil Ages","","chaperon-craquelure","Over the decades oil paint stiffens, can yellow and grows more see-through, so changes the painter covered may start to show. · A fine net of cracks covers the face, splitting the skin tones into small irregular islands. · The cracks run through light and shadow alike: the whole paint layer stiffened and shrank as it aged, and split. · Diego Velázquez · Philip IV · about 1628 · Earlier outlines of the king's legs and cloak have begun to show through the top layer of paint. · One study found that oil which yellows in the dark mostly loses that yellow after a short spell in daylight."],[8,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/how-oil-ages.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Philip IV","Diego Velázquez","thumb/velazquez-philip-iv","about 1628 · Earlier outlines of the king's legs and cloak have begun to show through the top layer of paint. · How Oil Ages"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","Pigments and Materials","Pigments and Materials","","amenhotep-palette","The earliest colors were dug from the ground or burned, and Egyptian blue, fired in a furnace, is the oldest known synthetic pigment. Most laboratory-made colors arrived after 1800; in 1841 an American painter, John Goffe Rand, devised the collapsible metal paint tube."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/whites.html","Pigments and Materials","Whites","","pm-whites","Until about 1900 the painter's main white was lead white, produced by hanging strips of lead over vinegar until the fumes turned them to a white crust. · On the same striped backing, the stripes still show through zinc white but vanish under lead and titanium white. · Zinc white reached artists in the 1830s, first as a watercolor, and titanium white only in the 1920s, so older oil paintings lean on lead white. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Self Portrait at the Age of 63 · 1669 · On the lit cheek, lead white tinted with ochres stands up in a thick layer over brown paint below. · Lead white is toxic; in 1978 the United States outlawed lead in paint for homes."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/whites.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Self Portrait at the Age of 63","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-self-portrait-63","1669 · On the lit cheek, lead white tinted with ochres stands up in a thick layer over brown paint below. · Whites"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blacks.html","Pigments and Materials","Blacks","","pm-blacks","Traditional blacks take their color from carbon, collected as soot from a flame or left behind when bones or vine twigs are charred. · Lamp black, gathered from a smoky flame, is soft and slightly blue, and oils left in it can keep linseed oil from drying properly. · What tubes sell as ivory black is almost always bone black: calcium phosphate from the bone, with carbon only part of the mix. · Édouard Manet · Music in the Tuileries Gardens · 1862 · Manet built the dark hats and coats from ivory black, tinted with cobalt blue and an orange-brown ochre. · In his Incredulity of Saint Thomas (1502–04), Cima da Conegliano used vine black for a cool gray drapery and bone black for warmer areas."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blacks.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Music in the Tuileries Gardens","Édouard Manet","thumb/manet-music-tuileries","1862 · Manet built the dark hats and coats from ivory black, tinted with cobalt blue and an orange-brown ochre. · Blacks"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/earth-pigments.html","Pigments and Materials","Earth Pigments","","pm-earths","Yellow and red earths owe their color to iron minerals mixed into clay and quartz. · The yellow is goethite, an iron mineral that holds water in its structure. · Fire drives the water out of goethite and leaves hematite, the red; people were already reddening ochre this way about 40,000 years ago. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Self-Portrait · 1659 · Analysis found a palette of ochres in red, yellow and brown, plus lead white and carbon black and just traces of vermilion. · In South Africa's Blombos Cave, people were grinding red ochre into a paint mixture about 100,000 years ago."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/earth-pigments.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","1659 · Analysis found a palette of ochres in red, yellow and brown, plus lead white and carbon black and just traces of vermilion. · Earth Pigments"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/reds.html","Pigments and Materials","Reds","","cochineal-plantation","A red could be a mineral, such as vermilion, or a lake: dye from a root or an insect fixed onto a colorless powder so it can be used as paint. · The cactus rows are a farm for the cochineal insect, which feeds on the pads. · The red dye sits inside the insects' bodies, so they are picked off by hand. · At the left, the harvest is laid out on a mat to cure. · Titian · Venus and Adonis · about 1555–60 · Adonis wears both kinds of red: vermilion in the orange-red sleeve, a lake in the pinker tunic. · Titian's lake was probably extracted from scraps of red-dyed fabric, and its dye has been matched mainly to cochineal."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/reds.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Venus and Adonis","Titian","thumb/titian-venus-adonis","about 1555–60 · Adonis wears both kinds of red: vermilion in the orange-red sleeve, a lake in the pinker tunic. · Reds"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blues.html","Pigments and Materials","Blues","","sowerby-lapis-azurite","Before 1700 a painter could choose from only a few blues, and two of them were crushed minerals. · Left: lapis lazuli, which yields ultramarine; powdered on its own it comes out grayish, so painters worked the blue out of a paste of wax and resin. · Right: azurite, a copper ore with darker, blocky crystals; its green patches are a reminder that azurite can turn green. · Johannes Vermeer · Girl with a Pearl Earring · about 1665 · The blue of the turban is top-quality natural ultramarine, lightened with lead white in the light and mixed with chalk in the shade. · In fifteenth-century Florence the best ultramarine might cost as much as gold leaf, so painters usually kept it for the most important figure."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blues.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Girl with a Pearl Earring","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-girl-pearl-earring","about 1665 · The blue of the turban is top-quality natural ultramarine, lightened with lead white in the light and mixed with chalk in the shade. · Blues"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/greens.html","Pigments and Materials","Greens","","pm-greens","Many old greens came from copper: malachite, a crushed mineral, and verdigris, made by exposing copper to vinegar. · The verdigris swatch is half blue-green and half brown, because copper greens can darken in light. · Of the newer greens, emerald green was a vivid copper-and-arsenic compound, while viridian, made from chromium, is cool in tone and very stable. · Paolo Uccello · The Battle of San Romano · probably about 1438–40 · The hedge behind the knights started out green; the museum records that it has turned nearly black. · Cennino Cennini, in his painters' handbook of about 1400, told painters to keep verdigris and lead white apart, calling them \"mortal enemies\"."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/greens.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Battle of San Romano","Paolo Uccello","thumb/uccello-battle-san-romano","probably about 1438–40 · The hedge behind the knights started out green; the museum records that it has turned nearly black. · Greens"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/yellows.html","Pigments and Materials","Yellows","","sowerby-orpiment-crocoite","The most brilliant yellows before 1800 were often poisonous, and one of the vivid new yellows of the 1800s grew out of a red-orange ore. · Left: orpiment, an arsenic mineral, bright yellow where it is pure and gray where iron pyrites runs through it. · Right: crocoite, natural lead chromate; in its red-orange crystals the chemist Vauquelin found chromium in 1797, and by 1809 he had made the same compound in the laboratory as chrome yellow. · Vincent van Gogh · Sunflowers · 1888 · Chrome yellows in several shades, from a pale lemon to a deep orange, supply most of the painting's yellows and oranges. · Cennini says orpiment looks \"like gold in colour\" and tells painters not to let it near their mouths."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/yellows.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-sunflowers","1888 · Chrome yellows in several shades, from a pale lemon to a deep orange, supply most of the painting's yellows and oranges. · Yellows"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/egg-tempera.html","Pigments and Materials","Egg Tempera","","pm-tempera","Egg tempera uses yolk to hold the pigment, and it was the standard paint for Italian panels through the Middle Ages and early Renaissance. · The sphere has no smooth blends anywhere: egg paint dries quickly, so its form is built from short, separate marks. · On the sphere, tightly packed strokes read as shadow and open spacing as light. · Giotto · Madonna and Child · about 1310–15 · Under the flesh lies a green underpaint for the shadows; fine hatching and wider strokes then round out the forms. · By the late 1400s Italian painters often combined tempera with oil, and after about 1500 tempera became less common."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/egg-tempera.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Madonna and Child","Giotto","thumb/giotto-madonna-child","about 1310–15 · Under the flesh lies a green underpaint for the shadows; fine hatching and wider strokes then round out the forms. · Egg Tempera"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fresco.html","Pigments and Materials","Fresco","","pm-fresco","In fresco the color goes onto lime plaster that is still damp, so it ends up inside the wall instead of on it. · On the left, carbon dioxide from the air slowly turns the lime back into something like stone, locking the grains in. · On the right, color on dry plaster has no lime to bind it, so it needs a binder of its own and wears less well; a flake has come loose. · Spanish (Castile) · Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza · after 1200 · This lion first decorated a monastery wall; in the early 1900s it was removed and fixed to canvas. · Plaster was laid one day's work at a time; each such patch is called a giornata."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fresco.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza","Spanish (Castile)","thumb/arlanza-lion","after 1200 · This lion first decorated a monastery wall; in the early 1900s it was removed and fixed to canvas. · Fresco"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/oil.html","Pigments and Materials","Oil","","stradanus-color-olivi","Oil paint holds its pigment in a drying oil, most often linseed, which sets slowly by absorbing oxygen. · At the right, assistants grind colors on slabs, the step where pigment was worked into the binder. · The Latin caption credits \"master Eyck\" with inventing oil color, a legend: oil was in use centuries earlier. · Rogier van der Weyden · The Magdalen Reading · about 1435 · Tests identified linseed oil; the lips melt together where wet paint met wet paint, and the brush pulled through the fur trim before it dried. · Oil also makes many pigments translucent, so thin layers of color can be glazed one over another."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/oil.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Magdalen Reading","Rogier van der Weyden","thumb/vanderweyden-magdalen-reading","about 1435 · Tests identified linseed oil; the lips melt together where wet paint met wet paint, and the brush pulled through the fur trim before it dried. · Oil"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/watercolor-and-gouache.html","Pigments and Materials","Watercolor and Gouache","","pm-watercolor","Watercolor is pigment bound in gum arabic and thinned with water; its brightness comes from the paper beneath. · In the thin wash, light slips between the grains, bounces off the white paper and comes back tinted. · Gouache carries more pigment for its gum, so light stops at the surface and the paper is hidden. · Winslow Homer · The Return, Tynemouth · 1881 · The soft pinks of sky and boat were painted with lightfast pigments and have survived; many of Homer's other watercolors have lost washes like them."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/watercolor-and-gouache.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Return, Tynemouth","Winslow Homer","thumb/homer-return-tynemouth","1881 · The soft pinks of sky and boat were painted with lightfast pigments and have survived; many of Homer's other watercolors have lost washes like them. · Watercolor and Gouache"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/pastel.html","Pigments and Materials","Pastel","","pm-pastel","A pastel stick is pigment and a pale filler like chalk, barely held together by a little weak gum. · Grains sit loose on the peaks and in the hollows of the paper's rough surface, with almost nothing binding them. · Loose powder scatters light, which makes pastel look soft and velvety, and a finger can smear it. · Jean-Étienne Liotard · Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age · 1755–56 · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; on vellum, a smooth prepared skin, he blended with a stump, a pointed tool, and this portrait comes as close to that ideal as any he made. · A pastel portrait needed no drying time and fewer sittings, and the medium became fashionable after Rosalba Carriera's stay in Paris in 1720–21."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/pastel.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age","Jean-Étienne Liotard","thumb/liotard-maria-frederike","1755–56 · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; on vellum, a smooth prepared skin, he blended with a stump, a pointed tool, and this portrait comes as close to that ideal as any he made. · Pastel"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/encaustic.html","Pigments and Materials","Encaustic","","pm-encaustic","Encaustic paint is pigment carried in beeswax; the word comes from the Greek for \"burnt in\". · Warm wax brushes on like paint and stiffens as it cools, which is why most encaustic painters kept their colors hot. · Going back over cooled strokes with a heated tool softens them again so they can be smoothed. · Roman Egypt · Portrait of a young woman in red · 90–120 CE · The wax went directly onto a board of linden only millimeters thick, and a hot tool left rows of parallel marks beside the nose. · Roughly 1,000 portraits like this survive from Roman Egyptian mummies, most from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/encaustic.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of a young woman in red","Roman Egypt","thumb/fayum-young-woman-red","90–120 CE · The wax went directly onto a board of linden only millimeters thick, and a hot tool left rows of parallel marks beside the nose. · Encaustic"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/wood-panels.html","Pigments and Materials","Wood Panels","","pm-panel","Between the 1200s and the early 1500s most surviving movable paintings were done on wood, either a single board or several joined with glue and dowels. · Wood absorbs and releases moisture as the air turns damp or dry, so the boards keep swelling and shrinking. · Over centuries the swelling and shrinking can bow a panel and split it, loosening the paint on its face. · Rogier van der Weyden · Portrait of a Lady · about 1460 · Its single oak board has 265 tree rings, the last from 1438; allowing for lost rings and seasoning, it was probably painted no earlier than about 1463. · Restorers once planed bowed panels down and glued wooden grids to their backs; today's conservators treat them more gently."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/wood-panels.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of a Lady","Rogier van der Weyden","thumb/vanderweyden-portrait-lady","about 1460 · Its single oak board has 265 tree rings, the last from 1438; allowing for lost rings and seasoning, it was probably painted no earlier than about 1463. · Wood Panels"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/canvas.html","Pigments and Materials","Canvas","","encyclopedie-peinture-pl4-chassis","Canvas is strong woven cloth, usually linen, stretched taut across a wooden frame. · From behind, a bar across the middle and braces at the corners hold the frame square against the pull of the cloth. · Close vertical hatching stands for the stretched cloth. · Titian · The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross · about 1540–45 · Nearly 9 1/2 feet across, it is one unseamed length of patterned twill, when most large canvases before the 1800s were stitched from strips. · Canvas weighs less than wood, and Venetian painters made the change, away from panels and walls, between about 1450 and 1550, though historians debate why."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/canvas.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross","Titian","thumb/titian-vendramin-family","about 1540–45 · Nearly 9 1/2 feet across, it is one unseamed length of patterned twill, when most large canvases before the 1800s were stitched from strips. · Canvas"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/grounds.html","Pigments and Materials","Grounds","","pm-layers","A ground is the first coat on a panel or canvas, giving paint a smooth, even surface to cling to; where the paint is thin, its color shows through. · Here the ground is gesso, gypsum bound with glue, the ground of Italian panels, sanded until it was glassy. · The thin dark line just above it is the underdrawing, set down on the ground before any color. · Peter Paul Rubens · Samson and Delilah · about 1609–10 · On top of a white chalk ground Rubens streaked a yellow-brown layer, then left that streaky layer uncovered in Delilah's sleeve folds to serve as a warm half-shadow. · After about 1600 warm brown or red grounds became common; in the 1800s many painters, the Impressionists most of all, returned to white."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/grounds.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Samson and Delilah","Peter Paul Rubens","thumb/rubens-samson-delilah","about 1609–10 · On top of a white chalk ground Rubens streaked a yellow-brown layer, then left that streaky layer uncovered in Delilah's sleeve folds to serve as a warm half-shadow. · Grounds"],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fading-and-darkening.html","Pigments and Materials","Fading and Darkening","","pm-fading","Some pigments shift color over the years as they react with light, with the air or with the oil that binds them. · Smalt is a blue glass; potassium leaches out of it into the oil, the blue is lost and the oil yellows, turning the paint gray-brown. · The white turning nearly black is lead white reacting with polluted air, which happens mostly on paper and in water-based paint. · Vincent van Gogh · The Bedroom · 1889 · Analysts found specks of red lake in the paint of the light-blue doors, and geranium lake, which has probably faded, in the floor. · Paint shielded from light can keep its first color: in the Amsterdam version, a stroke the frame has hidden is still lilac."],[9,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fading-and-darkening.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Bedroom","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-bedroom","1889 · Analysts found specks of red lake in the paint of the light-blue doors, and geranium lake, which has probably faded, in the floor. · Fading and Darkening"],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","Post-Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","vangogh-starry-night","Structure, feeling, flat colour and pattern after Impressionism · Post-Impressionism is the label given, after the fact, to painters working in France from about 1884 to 1906 who started from Impressionism and went past it. They kept its bright colour and visible brushwork, and used them for something other than recording light: solid structure, strong feeling, flat colour, or pattern. · Vincent van Gogh · The Starry Night · 1889"],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/bathers-at-asnieres.html","Post-Impressionism","Bathers at Asnières","Georges Seurat","seurat-bathers","Georges Seurat · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 79 1/4 × 118 in. The National Gallery, London · Georges Seurat (French, 1859–1891). The whole surface is made of small separate touches of paint, and the figures hold as still as statues. · Short crisscross strokes cover the grass, water and sky, so nothing is blended smooth. · Every figure is seen in profile or straight from behind, and the group is spaced along the bank from the lower left corner up to the far right. · The light is hazy and even, with no hard shadows anywhere. · Rejected by the official Salon and shown instead at the first Salon des Indépendants, a jury-free exhibition, in 1884. Seurat later reworked the orange hat in the dot technique."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-circus.html","Post-Impressionism","The Circus","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus","Georges Seurat · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 73 1/4 × 59 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The colour is built entirely from small dots, and the rising curves of the ring are set against the straight rows of the stands. · Dots of red, yellow and blue sit side by side on a white ground, and the eye mixes them at a distance. · The horse, rider and clowns are all arcs and spirals; the seats and audience behind are a grid of straight lines. · White dominates, so the three colours read as bright accents. · A dark blue border is painted on the canvas itself, part of the picture rather than a frame. · Shown unfinished at the Salon des Indépendants in March 1891; Seurat died days after the opening."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-basket-of-apples.html","Post-Impressionism","The Basket of Apples","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-basket","Paul Cézanne · about 1893 · Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 31 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Paul Cézanne (French, 1839–1906). The table does not line up: its front edge sits lower on the left of the cloth than on the right, as if seen from two places at once. · The tabletop rises steeply toward the viewer, so the fruit seems about to roll forward. · The basket tilts and the bottle leans, and neither is corrected. · Each apple is a solid ball of colour, shaded from green through orange to red in broad flat strokes. · Dark blue outlines run around the bottle, the cloth folds and the plate. · Shown at Cézanne's first one-man exhibition, at the Vollard gallery in Paris, 1895."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-card-players.html","Post-Impressionism","The Card Players","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-card-players","Paul Cézanne · 1890–92 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 32 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The men are built like solid blocks, with cylinders for arms and bodies and the hats as plain dark shapes on top. · The three seated players form a stable, balanced group around the table, and nobody moves. · Coats and sleeves are shaded in broad planes of blue, brown and cream, not in soft gradations. · The wall behind is loosely scrubbed in pale blue and lilac, so the figures stand out as the solid parts. · The four pipes on the wall are each a few dark strokes with a touch of white on the bowl, set straight onto the scrubbed blue. · The earliest of five pictures of card players; each sitter, a local farm worker, posed alone for studies that were then combined."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/mont-sainte-victoire.html","Post-Impressionism","Mont Sainte-Victoire","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-sainte-victoire","Paul Cézanne · 1902–04 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 36 1/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · The valley and the sky are both made of patches of parallel brushstrokes, and colour alone tells which is near and which is far. · Green and ochre patches in the lower half are each a cluster of short strokes running the same way. · The mountain and the sky share the same violets and blues, so the peak almost dissolves into the air. · The darkest greens and browns gather along the bottom edge, and the picture lightens step by step up to the pale peak. · A broken dark line marks the mountain's outer edge and a few roofs; most other shapes have no drawn edge at all. · The mountain stands near Aix-en-Provence, Cézanne's home town, and he painted it again and again in his last years."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-large-bathers.html","Post-Impressionism","The Large Bathers","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-bathers","Paul Cézanne · 1900–06 · Oil on canvas, 82 3/4 × 98 3/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · The bending tree trunks form a huge arch over the figures, and the two groups of bathers fit inside as a triangle. · The trunks lean in from both sides and meet near the top, locking the composition into a pointed arch. · The bathers are simplified to a few planes each, with faces barely marked. · Patches of bare canvas show through across the sky and the bodies. · The same blues run through the sky, the water and the shadows on the bodies. · The largest and last of Cézanne's bathers pictures, left unfinished."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-potato-eaters.html","Post-Impressionism","The Potato Eaters","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-potato-eaters","Vincent van Gogh · 1885 · Oil on canvas, 32 1/4 × 45 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation) · Vincent van Gogh (Dutch, 1853–1890). Before the bright colour came the opposite: a dark room lit by one lamp, painted in earth colours. · The single oil lamp is the only light source, and every face and hand is modelled by its glow. · The palette is browns, greys and muddy greens, with hardly a touch of clear blue or red. · Faces and hands are deliberately coarse and knobbly, drawn with dark contours. · The paint is thick and the brushwork rough, with the strokes left visible in the dark. · Painted at Nuenen, in the Dutch countryside, as a showpiece; Van Gogh compared the earth colours to a dusty, unpeeled potato."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-night-cafe.html","Post-Impressionism","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 36 1/4 in. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Red walls clash against a green ceiling and billiard table, and the floorboards rush away in steep diagonals. · Blood red walls and a green ceiling and table, opposites on the colour wheel, fight each other across the room. · The floor is the largest area, a field of yellow strokes that makes the room feel tilted. · Each gaslamp is ringed with concentric strokes of yellow and orange, painting the glare itself. · The owner's white suit turns pale green and lemon yellow under the lamps. · Shows a café in Arles, the Alcazar, where Van Gogh took his meals; he wrote that the clashing colours express 'the terrible passions of humanity'."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/sunflowers.html","Post-Impressionism","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 36 1/4 × 28 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Almost the whole picture is one colour: yellow flowers in a yellow vase on a yellow table against a yellow wall. · The yellows run from pale lemon in the background through gold to deep ochre in the seed heads. · The seed heads are built up in thick ridges of paint, so they stand off the canvas. · A thin blue line marks the table edge and a band around the vase; apart from the green stems and leaves, nothing else breaks the yellows. · The background is pale yellow, brushed thin in short crossing strokes, against the heavy paint of the heads. · Painted to decorate the house at Arles before Gauguin's visit, one of a series; the flowers run from fresh bud to withered head."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-starry-night.html","Post-Impressionism","The Starry Night","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-starry-night","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 29 × 36 1/4 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · The sky is made of long curling strokes that spiral and roll across the canvas, each one visible. · Two great spirals turn in the centre of the sky, built from short strokes laid end to end. · Each star and the moon sits inside a ring of pale strokes. · The cypress rises as a dark flame, painted in strokes that lick upward. · The village below is small and still, with straight little houses that set off the churning sky. · Based on the view from Van Gogh's window at the asylum of Saint-Rémy, painted in daytime sessions; the village was added from other views."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/self-portrait.html","Post-Impressionism","Self-Portrait","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-self-portrait-1889","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 21 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The same short curved strokes cover the face, the jacket and the background, so the man and the wall behind him move together. · The background is a field of swirling curls in pale green and blue, with no flat area anywhere. · The strokes in the hair and beard run in the same curling rhythm as the background. · Cool greens and turquoise fill the picture, and the orange hair and beard are the main warm note. · The eyes are the only still point, painted in sharp focus. · One of more than forty self-portraits Van Gogh made in about ten years; here he is dressed in a suit instead of the work clothes he usually painted himself in."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/vision-of-the-sermon-jacob-wrestling-with-the-angel.html","Post-Impressionism","Vision of the Sermon (Jacob Wrestling with the Angel)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-vision","Paul Gauguin · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 35 3/4 in. Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh · Paul Gauguin (French, 1848–1903). The ground is a flat field of pure red, and every shape sits on it inside a dark outline. · The red runs almost unbroken from edge to edge, with no shading and no grass. · The white Breton bonnets are flat shapes with hard edges, outlined in dark blue. · A tree trunk cuts the picture on a diagonal, separating the praying women from the small wrestlers. · The cow and the wrestlers are small, as if far away, but the flat red gives them no distance from the large heads in front. · Breton women are praying after a sermon about Jacob wrestling the angel; Gauguin wrote that the fight exists only in their imagination."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-talisman.html","Post-Impressionism","The Talisman","Paul Sérusier","serusier-talisman","Paul Sérusier · 1888 · Oil on wood, 10 3/4 × 8 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Paul Sérusier (French, 1864–1927). The landscape is reduced to patches of pure colour, with almost no drawing left. · Yellow, orange, blue, violet and green sit as separate blocks, each a single unmixed tone. · The trees at upper left, a red path and a blue mill at right can still be read, but only just. · Reflections in the water below are the same flat patches as the trees above them, with no ripple or blur. · Dark brown and violet masses at the right balance the bright yellow, and nothing is shaded. · Painted at Pont-Aven in October 1888 under Gauguin's direction; shown in Paris to the painters who formed the Nabis, for whom the panel became a talisman."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/where-do-we-come-from-what-are-we-where-are-we-going.html","Post-Impressionism","Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-where","Paul Gauguin · 1897–98 · Oil on canvas, 54 3/4 × 147 1/2 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · The picture is a long band that reads from right to left, a sleeping baby at one end and an old woman at the other, in muted blue-green. · Figures stand or sit in a row across the front, with the landscape behind them. · Deep blue and green fill most of the ground, and the bodies are ochre and brown with little shading. · The two upper corners are yellow, like the worn edges of an old wall painting. · A blue idol at the left stands stiff and symmetrical, in a different pose from everyone else. · Painted in Tahiti; Gauguin wanted the effect of a wall painting, and first showed it at Vollard's Paris gallery in 1898."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/at-the-moulin-rouge.html","Post-Impressionism","At the Moulin Rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-moulin-rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–95 · Oil on canvas, 48 1/2 × 55 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec (French, 1864–1901). A railing slices across the lower left, and the face cut off by the right edge is turned green by electric light. · The woman at the right is lit from below in acid green, with her hair bright yellow. · The orange railing runs diagonally through the foreground, blocking the viewer's way in. · The paint is thin and streaky, with the brown of the canvas showing through the figures and floor. · Black top hats and dark coats make flat silhouettes against the green wall behind. · Lautrec's own friends fill the table at the Moulin Rouge; he is in the centre background beside his cousin."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/divan-japonais.html","Post-Impressionism","Divan Japonais","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-divan","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–93 · Lithograph printed in four colours, 31 3/4 × 24 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The seated woman is a single flat black shape, and the singer on stage is cut off at the neck by the top edge. · The black dress and hat are one unshaded silhouette; only the orange hair and a sliver of face are picked out. · The stage, the orchestra and the audience are flat planes of yellow, grey and olive with thick outlines. · The performer at upper left is known only from her long black gloves, with her head outside the picture. · The lettering is drawn by hand and sits inside the design rather than in a separate band. · An advertisement for a Montmartre café-concert; the spectator is Jane Avril, the performer on stage Yvette Guilbert, two stars of the district."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/woman-in-a-striped-dress.html","Post-Impressionism","Woman in a Striped Dress","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-striped","Édouard Vuillard · 1895 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 23 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Édouard Vuillard (French, 1868–1940). The woman's dress, the flowers and the wall are all pattern, so the figure nearly disappears into the room. · Red and white stripes on the sleeves are painted in loose dabs that match the dabs of the flowers beside them. · The background is dark red and mottled, the same red as the stripes, so there is no clear edge between dress and wall. · Faces are blurred to a few strokes while the patterns are sharp. · The palette is held to reds, whites, greens and yellows across the whole canvas. · Made in 1895 for Thadée Natanson, who published the magazine La Revue blanche, as one of five decorations for his home; the red-haired woman may be his wife Misia."],[10,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-sleeping-gypsy.html","Post-Impressionism","The Sleeping Gypsy","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-sleeping-gypsy","Henri Rousseau · 1897 · Oil on canvas, 51 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Henri Rousseau (French, 1844–1910). Every edge is hard and every surface smooth, with no visible brushwork and no haze in the distance. · The lion, the sleeper and the mandolin are outlined cleanly, like cut-outs placed on the sand. · The far mountains are as crisp as the foreground, so the distance does not soften. · The stripes of the robe are painted one by one in flat, even bands. · The sky is a smooth gradient from dark blue to pale, with the moon a plain white disc. · Shown in Paris at the 1897 Salon des Indépendants."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism.html","Realism · Overview","What to Look For","","millet-gleaners","Everyday life, painted as it looks · Realism is a nineteenth-century movement that painted subjects from everyday life in a naturalistic way. Before it, Western art was led by the academic ideal of history painting. The French novelist Champfleury coined the name in the 1840s, and the painter Gustave Courbet became the movement's best-known example. · Jean-François Millet · The Gleaners · 1857"],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Realism","Forest of Fontainebleau","Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 69 1/4 × 95 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot (French, 1796–1875). Browns, olive greens and dull golds fill a real forest with no grand ruin or classical temple. · Light falls in patches on a sandy bank in the middle distance. · The trees at the right are a dark mass, with single leaves picked out in light. · The still, dark pool reflects the overhanging rock. · The figure in the lower left is small, a light accent in a large landscape. · Made in the studio from sketches and studies painted outdoors, and shown at the Salon, the official exhibition of the French Academy."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/rue-transnonain-15-april-1834.html","Realism","Rue Transnonain, 15 April 1834","Honoré Daumier","daumier-rue-transnonain","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 11 1/4 × 17 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Honoré Daumier (French, 1808–1879). A dead man lies foreshortened across the floor, his legs thrust toward the lower left. · The pale body and bedding stand out against a dark doorway at the left. · Tones run from near-white to deep black, with the grain of the drawing stone showing through. · Dark blood stains mark the floor and the nightshirt. · Bodies are cut off at the left and right edges, so the scene runs past the frame. · A lithograph, printed from a drawing made with a greasy crayon on a flat stone. The print records an event during the riots of that April, when government troops fired on the people living in a building."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/a-burial-at-ornans.html","Realism","A Burial at Ornans","Gustave Courbet","courbet-burial-at-ornans","Gustave Courbet · 1849–50 · Oil on canvas, 124 × 263 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Gustave Courbet (French, 1819–1877). Villagers are painted at life size on a canvas about 22 feet wide. · Most of the crowd is a band of black clothing; white collars, caps and handkerchiefs break it up. · Faces fill one long, packed row, and no single figure is set apart as a hero. · The low grey cliffs behind run parallel to the row of mourners. · The paint is heavy and the blacks are deep, with brushwork left visible. · The crucifix is the only form that rises above the line of cliffs. · The museum calls the canvas a manifesto of a new aesthetic, Realism. The sitters were people of Ornans."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-sower.html","Realism","The Sower","Jean-François Millet","millet-sower","Jean-François Millet · 1850 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 32 1/2 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Jean-François Millet (French, 1814–1875). The face is lost in shadow under the hat, and the body is a dark mass against a grey sky. · One heavy figure strides downhill and fills almost the whole canvas. · Earth browns cover the hillside and the shirt. · The blue trousers are the strongest color against the browns. · The far horizon glows pale yellow behind a tiny team of oxen. · The striding legs form a wide diagonal, which gives the figure its forward push. · The figure scatters wheat seed from a bag slung over the shoulder."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-horse-fair.html","Realism","The Horse Fair","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-horse-fair","Rosa Bonheur · 1852–55 · Oil on canvas, 96 1/4 × 199 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Rosa Bonheur (French, 1822–1899). A stream of horses surges from left to right. · White horses at the center stand out against dark and chestnut ones. · Two horses rear at the center, a black one behind a white one, at the highest point of the moving line. · Muscles, hooves and manes are painted in close detail. · Dust rises from the dirt road and softens the legs and shadows. · A dark band of trees and a stormy sky hold the top of the picture. · A horse market on a Paris boulevard. The painting first appeared at the Salon of 1853 and was retouched by the artist in 1855."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-painters-studio.html","Realism","The Painter's Studio","Gustave Courbet","courbet-painters-studio","Gustave Courbet · 1854–55 · Oil on canvas, 142 1/4 × 235 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The upper half of the canvas is nearly empty, washed in thin browns and greys above a crowded floor. · Two crowds flank a seated painter, whose easel and model form a lit center. · The easel holds a bright landscape, the clearest color in the room. · The model's pale body and the white cloth heaped at her feet make one tall light shape beside the easel. · Most figures are dark shapes against a dim, brown wall. · Made for the 1855 world's fair in Paris and rejected. The painter then showed the canvas in a Pavilion of Realism built at his own expense."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gleaners.html","Realism","The Gleaners","Jean-François Millet","millet-gleaners","Jean-François Millet · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 32 3/4 × 43 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Three stooping figures make a low run of curves across the foreground. · The three caps repeat red, blue and yellow. · Low, late light casts long shadows and makes the figures look solid, like sculpture. · The harvest lies far behind, where haystacks and a loaded cart are pale and small. · Gleaners gathered the ears of grain left after the harvest. The painting was shown at the Salon."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-angelus.html","Realism","The Angelus","Jean-François Millet","millet-angelus","Jean-François Millet · 1857–59 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 26 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two figures stand still and upright, the man straight and the woman bowed, against an empty field. · Heads and shoulders are dark against a glowing sky; the horizon crosses both figures at chest height. · Light picks out the man's open collar, the woman's pink cuffs and the sack on the wheelbarrow. · A pitchfork at the left and a wheelbarrow at the right frame the pair. · A small church spire sits on the horizon just right of the woman's shoulder. · A man and a woman stop the potato harvest to recite the angelus, a prayer said when a church bell rings."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-old-musician.html","Realism","The Old Musician","Édouard Manet","manet-old-musician","Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 73 3/4 × 97 3/4 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Édouard Manet (French, 1832–1883). Figures are broad, flat shapes with little modeling. · The figures stand and sit in a loose row and barely look at one another. · The boy's white shirt and pale trousers are the brightest area among the figures. · The ground and sky are brushed in broad, open strokes. · A tree at the upper left and the cloaked figure at the far right close the row. · The musician's red violin is the strongest single color. · The museum says the canvas shows homeless and marginalized people."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-third-class-carriage.html","Realism","The Third-Class Carriage","Honoré Daumier","daumier-third-class-carriage","Honoré Daumier · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 35 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Dark outlines drawn in paint shape the figures, and the canvas is left unfinished. · A faint grid of squaring lines shows through the paint. · The two women in front are lit; the boy and the passengers behind sit in shadow. · The carriage walls are one warm red-brown that surrounds everyone. · The bench back runs straight across the canvas and divides front from back. · Unfinished: the grid was for copying the design at another size. The museum notes a finished version in Ottawa."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-veteran-in-a-new-field.html","Realism","The Veteran in a New Field","Winslow Homer","homer-veteran-new-field","Winslow Homer · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 24 1/4 × 38 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Winslow Homer (American, 1836–1910). The picture is three bands: blue sky, a wall of golden wheat and cut stalks in front. · One figure, seen from behind, is set against the wheat. · Cut stalks are quick strokes of yellow and white that fan out across the foreground. · The curved blade of the scythe is a dark line across the gold. · A dark jacket and canteen lie on the ground at the lower right. · Completed after the end of the Civil War. The discarded Union jacket and canteen show that the farmer is a returned soldier."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-wave.html","Realism","The Wave","Gustave Courbet","courbet-the-wave","Gustave Courbet · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 36 1/2 in. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · Thick paint is pushed and scraped on, so the wave looks heavy and solid. · The wave is a dark green wall across the middle of the canvas. · Foam is laid on in thick white patches. · Rocks in the foreground are red-brown blocks of paint. · The sky is a low ceiling of brown-grey cloud with no blue. · No boat or figure gives a sense of scale. · One of about sixty pictures of storm waves on the Normandy coast, made between 1865 and 1870."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/barge-haulers-on-the-volga.html","Realism","Barge Haulers on the Volga","Ilya Repin","repin-barge-haulers","Ilya Repin · 1870–73 · Oil on canvas, 51 3/4 × 110 3/4 in. State Russian Museum, St Petersburg · Ilya Repin (1844–1930). Eleven men in harness form one long chain from the left toward the ship at the right. · Most heads bend down and forward under the strain of the harness. · One young figure near the center stands upright, the lightest and clearest face. · Ragged clothes are browns, greys and dull reds against pale sand. · The sand and sky are bright and flat, so the dark group stands out. · The ship's mast rises far above the bent heads. · The figures are based on portraits of real haulers, drawn on two trips to the Volga."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-champion-single-sculls-max-schmitt-in-a-single-scull.html","Realism","The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-champion-single-sculls","Thomas Eakins · 1871 · Oil on canvas, 32 1/4 × 46 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Thomas Eakins (American, 1844–1916). Clear, even daylight shows every detail at every distance. · The river is a smooth mirror of the sky and trees. · The bridges far behind are drawn as sharply as the boat in front. · The yellow scull and its oars make long, thin lines across the water. · Late-autumn trees are rust and brown against a cool blue sky. · A second rower sits in the middle distance, small and in the same clear light. · Believed to mark a rowing win by Max Schmitt in 1870. The painter shows himself as the rower in the middle distance."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-iron-rolling-mill.html","Realism","The Iron Rolling Mill","Adolph Menzel","menzel-iron-rolling-mill","Adolph Menzel · 1872–75 · Oil on canvas, 62 1/4 × 100 in. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Adolph Menzel (German, 1815–1905). Deep dark fills the factory, broken by a white-hot glow at the center and smaller fires behind. · Light from the hot iron falls on faces, arms and tongs nearby. · Beams, belts and wheels form a dark web overhead. · Workers at the edges wash and eat in near darkness. · Brushwork is quick and broken, with sparks as dabs of yellow. · Industrial labor, painted at a size once kept for history painting. The painter drew at a works in Upper Silesia in 1872."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gross-clinic.html","Realism","The Gross Clinic","Thomas Eakins","eakins-gross-clinic","Thomas Eakins · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 96 × 78 1/2 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts · Bright red on the surgeon's fingers and scalpel stands out against browns and blacks. · One strong light from above falls on the surgeon's head, his hand and the patient. · The tiers of students behind are dark brown and grey, almost lost in shadow. · The surgeon's forehead and white hair are the lightest part of the upper half. · A figure at the left shields her eyes, a dark shape turned away. · Instruments in the lower left are small, sharp highlights. · Made for the 1876 Centennial Exhibition; the art jury rejected it. Formally titled Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-song-of-the-lark.html","Realism","The Song of the Lark","Jules Breton","breton-song-of-the-lark","Jules Breton · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 43 1/2 × 33 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Jules Breton (French, 1827–1906). One upright figure fills most of the height, her head above a low horizon. · A red sun low on the horizon sends warm light across the field. · The face is lifted and lit, the clearest detail. · Bare feet and the dusty path are painted closely. · The sky fades from pink-orange to pale yellow. · A band of green field separates the bare ground from the village. · A peasant girl stops work to hear a lark, painted in an idealized style."],[11,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gulf-stream.html","Realism","The Gulf Stream","Winslow Homer","homer-gulf-stream","Winslow Homer · 1899 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/4 × 49 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Short, broken strokes of teal, navy and white build a rough sea around a small boat tilted on a diagonal. · Sharks circle in the foreground as grey and white shapes. · A waterspout rises at the right as a pale grey funnel. · A ship sits tiny and faint on the horizon at the left. · The broken stump of the mast juts from the bow at the left. · A Black man faces danger alone on a damaged boat. The painter reworked the canvas by 1906, the year the museum bought it."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo.html","Rococo · Overview","What to Look For","","fragonard-swing","France, Venice and Bavaria, from about 1717 to the 1770s · Rococo is the light, curving, pastel style of Paris, Venice and Bavaria in the decades after 1715. · Jean-Honoré Fragonard · The Swing · c. 1767–68"],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pilgrimage-to-the-island-of-cythera.html","Rococo","Pilgrimage to the Island of Cythera","Antoine Watteau","watteau-cythera","Antoine Watteau · 1717 · Oil on canvas, 50 3/4 × 76 1/2 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Antoine Watteau (French, 1684–1721). The air comes first: a hillside of couples dissolving into a hazy pink and blue distance. · The far shore, the lake and the mountains are a soft blue-grey haze, with no hard edge anywhere in the distance. · The trees are loose clusters of small touches, thin enough for the warm ground to show through. · The couples run in a chain from the statue at the right down to the boat at the left, so the eye drifts across the hill like a slow walk. · The silks are where the paint thickens, in small bright touches of rose, gold and white. · Delivered to the Paris Academy in 1717 as a reception piece and registered as a fête galante, an elegant party out of doors."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pierrot.html","Rococo","Pierrot","Antoine Watteau","watteau-pierrot","Antoine Watteau · c. 1718–19 · Oil on canvas, 72 1/2 × 61 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · White on white: a whole costume painted in thin, shimmering layers of satin. · The costume is many whites at once, cool blue-grey in the folds and warm cream on the sleeves, so the cloth reads as satin. · The figure stands as one pale column from collar to shoes, hands hanging, in front of a sky of patchy blue and cream. · The other actors are pushed down into a strip of heads at knee height, which leaves the white figure standing alone. · The brushwork in the hat, ruff and ribbons is quick and loose, with the edges left soft. · A stock character from the Italian comedy."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/mezzetin.html","Rococo","Mezzetin","Antoine Watteau","watteau-mezzetin","Antoine Watteau · c. 1718–20 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 17 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A small canvas built on one serpentine curve and a shower of broken strokes. · The body twists in a spiral: feet and knees pushed to the lower left, guitar across the lap, head thrown back to the upper right, one long S against the straight bench. · The pink, white and pale blue stripes of the costume are laid in with a small quick brush, each stroke left separate. · The pink of the cap, cape and rosettes is the strongest colour, set against dark green foliage. · A grey statue in the garden turns her back on the singer, a small joke placed in the shadow. · A comic character from the Italian stage, singing a serenade."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/gersaints-shopsign.html","Rococo","Gersaint's Shopsign","Antoine Watteau","watteau-gersaint","Antoine Watteau · 1720 · Oil on canvas, 64 1/4 × 120 1/2 in. Schloss Charlottenburg, Berlin · A shop becomes a shallow stage, the whole wide canvas kept in silver, rose and straw colour. · The room is a box open at the front, with the counter and walls running parallel to the picture plane. · A glazed doorway splits the canvas near the middle, and each half holds its own group of figures. · Several figures are seen from behind; the woman in rose silk at the left is the brightest shape in the room. · The figures are pale and cool, silvers, pinks and greys, against warm brown walls of gold-framed paintings stacked to the ceiling. · Painted as the sign for a Paris picture dealer's shop; at the left a portrait of Louis XIV is lowered into a crate."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-triumph-of-venus.html","Rococo","The Triumph of Venus","François Boucher","boucher-triumph-venus","François Boucher · 1740 · Oil on canvas, 51 1/4 × 63 3/4 in. Nationalmuseum, Stockholm · François Boucher (French, 1703–1770). The composition is one great spiral of pink flesh, striped silk and blue-green sea. · A striped pink-and-white cloth loops across the top of the canvas and a chain of bodies curves around below, so nothing in the picture comes to rest. · The colour is held to blue and pink: blue sky, blue drapery and sea against pink skin and rose cloth. · The flesh is smooth and pearly, with blended edges, against water painted in short curling strokes. · The dark cloud and cliff at the upper right are the one heavy mass; the cloud's edge melts into the pale sky while the rock stays sharp. · Venus carried over the waves by sea nymphs and tritons; shown at the Paris Salon of 1740."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/le-dejeuner.html","Rococo","Le Déjeuner","François Boucher","boucher-dejeuner","François Boucher · 1739 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A Rococo interior of curling gilt frames, clock and console, with a breakfast going on inside it. · The clock case, the candle brackets, the mirror crest and the gilt table at the far left are all C- and S-curves. · Straight lines come only from the window bars, the mirror rails and the floorboards, and they set off the curls. · A tall window at the left lights the group; the mirror reflects a doorway, which makes the shallow room read as deep. · Colour comes in small saturated patches, a red lacquer table, a red cape and a blue dress, against gold and brown. · A Chinese figure sits on the shelf behind the man: the taste for chinoiserie was part of the style."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/madame-de-pompadour.html","Rococo","Madame de Pompadour","François Boucher","boucher-pompadour","François Boucher · 1756 · Oil on canvas, 80 3/4 × 63 1/2 in. Alte Pinakothek, Munich · Pattern on pattern: roses on sea-green satin, against a gold curtain and a carved gilt mirror. · The green dress spreads across the lower half as one huge shape, and the room is arranged around it. · Pink roses, pink bows and lace are set along every edge of the dress, so the surface is never plain. · Gold answers green throughout: the curtain, the mirror frame and the clock against the dress and the wall. · The silk is painted in long smooth strokes, the roses and lace in small raised touches, so the handling changes with each material. · Louis XV's official mistress, shown with the books, letters, music and drawings of a cultivated life."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-swing.html","Rococo","The Swing","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-swing","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · c. 1767–68 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 1/4 in. The Wallace Collection, London · Jean-Honoré Fragonard (French, 1732–1806). One shaft of sunlight falls through the trees onto a pink dress. · The pink dress and the patch of pale sky at the upper left are the two brightest passages; the rest of the garden sinks into blue-green shadow. · The foliage is painted in loose, feathery touches, and the leaves at the edges dissolve into the light. · The ropes run down from the upper right to the seat, and the kicked leg and flying slipper carry the movement on to the left. · The dress is the most tightly worked part, its folds caught in small crisp strokes of white and pink. · A young woman on a swing, a man hidden in the bushes below, and a stone cupid with a finger to his lips."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-progress-of-love-the-meeting.html","Rococo","The Progress of Love: The Meeting","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-meeting","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · 1771–72 · Oil on canvas, 125 × 96 in. The Frick Collection, New York · A tall wall panel in which the garden takes most of the height and the figures are small at the bottom. · The two figures fill only the bottom third; above them a statue, an urn and a mass of trees rise to the top of the panel. · The trees are a dome of short green touches lit along their edges, against a sky blended smooth. · A white dress at the left and a red coat at the right pull against each other across the grey pedestal. · Roses tumble over the stone at both lower corners, loose and quickly painted. · One of a set of garden scenes painted for Madame du Barry's pavilion outside Paris, then returned to the painter."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/young-girl-reading.html","Rococo","Young Girl Reading","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-girl-reading","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · c. 1769 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Speed: a picture put down fast in lemon yellow and left showing. · Long loaded strokes run down the sleeve, across the bodice and over the cushion, none of them blended. · The shadows inside the white ruff are scratched into wet paint, drawn with the brush handle rather than painted. · A single yellow carries the picture, against a grey-brown wall and a lilac-pink cushion. · The profile and the hand are worked to a finish; everything else is left as sketch. · One of the fantasy figures, a set of quickly painted half-length figures in fancy dress."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/allegory-of-the-planets-and-continents.html","Rococo","Allegory of the Planets and Continents","Giovanni Battista Tiepolo","tiepolo-planets","Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · 1752 · Oil on canvas, 73 × 54 7/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Giovanni Battista Tiepolo (Italian, 1696–1770). A ceiling in miniature: figures on clouds seen from below around an almost empty pale sky. · The centre of the canvas is open sky in cream and pale blue; the figures crowd the four edges. · The figures are foreshortened as if seen from the floor far below, with legs and undersides toward the viewer. · The paint is thin and quick, with figures flicked in by a few strokes and the canvas tone left showing between them. · Warm gold gathers around the radiant figure left of centre; the rest is blue-grey with small notes of red. · The sketch presented in 1752 as the proposal for the staircase ceiling of the Würzburg palace."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/gustavus-hamilton-second-viscount-boyne-in-masquerade-costume.html","Rococo","Gustavus Hamilton, Second Viscount Boyne, in Masquerade Costume","Rosalba Carriera","carriera-boyne","Rosalba Carriera · 1730–31 · Pastel on paper, laid down on canvas, 22 1/4 × 16 7/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Rosalba Carriera (Italian, 1673–1757). Pastel gives the whole surface a soft, powdery skin. · The face is rubbed smooth, with no visible stroke; the blue cloak is dragged on in open strokes that show the grain of the paper. · One block of turquoise sits against a grey ground, with the black of the hat and lace veil around the head. · The pushed-up white mask and the lace at the throat are the two bright notes. · The bust fills the sheet, turned three-quarters, with nothing behind; the likeness is the whole picture. · An Irish visitor in Venetian Carnival dress: mask, veil, tricorne and short cape."],[12,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pilgrimage-church-of-wies-interior.html","Rococo","Pilgrimage Church of Wies, interior","Dominikus and Johann Baptist Zimmermann","zimmermann-wieskirche","Dominikus and Johann Baptist Zimmermann · 1745–54 · Stucco, fresco and gilding. Steingaden, Bavaria · Dominikus Zimmermann (German, 1685–1766) and Johann Baptist Zimmermann (German, 1680–1758). The same style built at full scale: white and gold stucco curling under a painted ceiling. · The walls and piers are white, and the gold ornament runs over them in scrolls that break across every straight edge. · The pulpit and the capitals are piled with stucco foliage and figures, so the architecture seems to grow rather than to be built. · Tall clear windows light the nave, and the white walls pick up the daylight. · The chancel narrows between red-marbled columns, and the colour deepens from the white nave toward the gilt altar. · The ceiling frescoes sit inside curling white frames, and the painted sky seems to open above the stucco. · A pilgrimage church in the Bavarian countryside, designed by one brother and decorated by the other."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","Romanticism · Overview","What to Look For","","friedrich-wanderer","Feeling, imagination and wild nature · Romanticism is the name given to a shift in art and literature that was at its height from about 1780 to 1830 and was felt long after. Artists in Britain, Spain, Germany, France and the United States turned away from classical restraint and put personal feeling, the workings of the mind and the natural world at the center of their pictures. · Caspar David Friedrich · Wanderer above the Sea of Fog · about 1817"],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-nightmare.html","Romanticism","The Nightmare","Henry Fuseli","fuseli-nightmare","Henry Fuseli · 1781 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 50 in. Detroit Institute of Arts · Henry Fuseli (Swiss, 1741–1825). A limp white body stretches across most of the width of a dark room. · The body makes one long curve, with head and arm hanging below the edge of the bed. · The strongest light falls on the white gown and bare arms; the creature above and the horse's head are brown and grey, half lost in shadow. · Red runs through the curtain behind and the cloth trailing to the floor. · The background gives no depth: curtain and wall merge into one dark red-brown. · First shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1782."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-ancient-of-days.html","Romanticism","The Ancient of Days","William Blake","blake-ancient-of-days","William Blake · 1794 · Color-printed relief etching with oil, watercolor and ink on paper, plate 9 1/4 × 6 1/2 in. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · William Blake (British, 1757–1827). A muscular figure drawn in firm outline crouches inside a glowing disk. · Dark contour lines mark each muscle of the arm and leg. · The disk is orange and yellow against black, ringed by cloud edged in red. · Fine straight rays fan out below the disk in gold. · The two legs of a compass open in straight lines toward the bottom corners. · Hair and beard stream sideways to the left in long parallel strands. · The frontispiece, or opening picture, of the artist's own book Europe a Prophecy."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-sleep-of-reason-produces-monsters.html","Romanticism","The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters","Francisco de Goya","goya-sleep-of-reason","Francisco de Goya · 1799 · Etching and aquatint, plate 8 1/4 × 6 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Francisco de Goya (Spanish, 1746–1828). The whole picture is built from black line and flat grey tone, with no color. · The background is an even, grainy grey. · Owls and bats are drawn in quick scratched lines, darker and looser toward the top. · The sleeper's coat and the wings of the owl above are the lightest areas, left nearly white. · The largest dark shape, a creature with spread wings, hangs near the top. · Words are written on the side of the desk, pale against the grey. · An etching with aquatint, a method that prints areas of tone. Plate 43 of Los Caprichos, a series of 80 plates."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-monk-by-the-sea.html","Romanticism","The Monk by the Sea","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Caspar David Friedrich · 1808–10 · Oil on canvas, 43 1/4 × 67 1/2 in. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Caspar David Friedrich (German, 1774–1840). Three horizontal bands, pale sand, dark sea and a huge sky, fill the canvas, and almost nothing else. · The sky takes up about three quarters of the height. · The single figure is tiny, a dark upright mark left of center. · No tree, boat or rock at the sides leads the eye in; the bands run off both edges. · The sky is darkest just above the sea, pale in the middle and blue at the top. · The artist worked on the canvas for about two years, and repeated repainting left the scene unusually empty."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-third-of-may-1808.html","Romanticism","The Third of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 105 1/2 × 136 1/2 in. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · All the light comes from one lantern on the ground, and it lands on a man in a white shirt. · The lantern is a cube of white and yellow set between the two groups. · The soldiers are a row of backs with no faces, joined into one block. · The rifles form a tight band of parallel lines pointing left. · Paint is loose: blood on the ground is smeared red, and faces are a few quick strokes. · The sky is flat black above a dull, half-lit hill. · The Prado's full title adds “in Madrid, or The Executions.”"],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.html","Romanticism","Wanderer above the Sea of Fog","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-wanderer","Caspar David Friedrich · about 1817 · Oil on canvas, 37 1/4 × 29 1/2 in. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Hard-edged brown rock stands against soft-edged fog in greys, pinks and whites. · Figure and rock together form a dark triangle rising from the bottom edge. · Distant ridges slope down from both sides and meet behind the figure's chest. · A single dark figure stands dead center. · The canvas is upright, taller than wide. · A figure seen from behind is called a Rückenfigur. The picture shows a person looking, not only the view."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-raft-of-the-medusa.html","Romanticism","The Raft of the Medusa","Théodore Géricault","gericault-raft-medusa","Théodore Géricault · 1818–19 · Oil on canvas, 193 1/4 × 282 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Théodore Géricault (French, 1791–1824). Bodies are piled into a pyramid that climbs from the lower left to a man waving a cloth at the upper right. · A second diagonal runs the other way, up the mast and its ropes to the left. · Raised arms all reach toward the upper right. · Flesh is modeled in strong light and deep shadow, like sculpture. · Color keeps to a narrow range: browns, dull greens and pale flesh. · A corner of the raft juts forward at the bottom edge. · The picture records the 1816 wreck of a frigate off Senegal. About 150 people went onto a raft, and 15 were alive when a ship found them 12 days later."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/saturn-devouring-his-son.html","Romanticism","Saturn Devouring His Son","Francisco de Goya","goya-saturn","Francisco de Goya · 1820–23 · Mixed technique, mural transferred to canvas, 56 1/2 × 32 in. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · The body is distorted: limbs are angular and out of proportion, with no smooth outline. · The eyes are white circles with black dots, the sharpest marks in the picture. · The figure is roughly brushed in a few earth colors and seems to come out of solid black. · Red is kept to the small body and the fingers that grip it. · Broad strokes show in the hair, thigh and knee. · No floor, wall or horizon places the figure anywhere. · Painted straight onto a wall of the artist's house and later moved to canvas, with much paint lost in the transfer."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-hay-wain.html","Romanticism","The Hay Wain","John Constable","constable-hay-wain","John Constable · 1821 · Oil on canvas, 51 1/4 × 73 in. The National Gallery, London · John Constable (British, 1776–1837). Small dabs of white paint flicker on the water and the leaves. · The clouds are built up in greys and whites, as solid as the trees. · Greens vary from near-black in the big trees to yellow-green in the far meadow. · Foliage is made of many small separate strokes, not smooth blended color. · The only bright red is a few small patches on the horses' harness. · The cart sits low and right of center, small against trees and sky. · The millpond is at Flatford on the River Stour; the work was done in the artist's London studio."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-death-of-sardanapalus.html","Romanticism","The Death of Sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix · 1827 · Oil on canvas, 154 1/4 × 195 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Eugène Delacroix (French, 1798–1863). Pale bodies are set next to dark ones across the whole canvas. · A great red bed runs on a diagonal from upper left to lower right, and bodies spill around it. · No single figure holds the center; the eye circles from group to group. · Warm reds, pinks and golds dominate against a brown-black background. · Jewels, gold vessels and harness glint in tiny highlights. · Space is shallow and tilted up. · The picture did not sell, and the artist held on to it for about 18 years."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/liberty-leading-the-people.html","Romanticism","Liberty Leading the People","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-liberty-leading","Eugène Delacroix · 1830 · Oil on canvas, 102 1/4 × 128 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · The red, white and blue of the flag are echoed in small notes across a picture that is mostly grey and brown. · Blue and red return in the kneeling figure's jacket and sash. · The woman with the flag stands highest, and the dead lie along the bottom edge. · Smoke hides most of the background, so the figures stand out against pale grey-white. · Smoke and sky are brushed freely; faces and hands are tighter. · Pale buildings show through the smoke at the right edge. · Painted from September to December and also called A Barricade."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/women-of-algiers-in-their-apartment.html","Romanticism","Women of Algiers in Their Apartment","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-women-algiers","Eugène Delacroix · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 70 3/4 × 90 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Pattern covers nearly every surface: tiles, carpets, cushions and clothes. · Shadows are colored, not grey: blue on the tiled wall, deep red in the curtain. · Light enters from the left and falls on the reclining woman's cheek and blouse; half her face stays in shadow. · Small touches of orange, red and blue sit side by side in the fabrics. · The red cupboard doors are the strongest warm color, set left of center. · Three seated figures form a low row; a standing figure closes the right edge as one tall vertical. · Bought by the state at the Salon, the official French art exhibition, in the year the canvas was painted."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","Romanticism","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 51 1/2 × 76 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Thomas Cole (American, 1801–1848). Dark storm and forest on the left give way to sunlit farmland on the right. · A broken tree at the left leans toward the upper left corner and frames the dark side. · The river makes one large loop in the lit half, pale and smooth. · Storm cloud is painted in grey sheets; the clearing sky is pale yellow and blue. · Fields are small neat patches of yellow and green; the forest is a mass of dark greens. · The viewpoint is high, looking down on the valley. · The river is the Connecticut. The artist painted himself at an easel among the rocks at the bottom."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Romanticism","The Fighting Temeraire","J.M.W. Turner","turner-fighting-temeraire","J.M.W. Turner · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 47 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · J.M.W. Turner (English, 1775–1851). The canvas splits into cool and warm: a pale ship against blue at the left, an orange and red sunset at the right. · The tall ship is painted thin and pale, barely darker than the sky behind. · The tug in front is dark brown and black, under a plume of rust-colored smoke. · The sunset is laid on in broad streaks of orange, yellow and red. · The water is a smooth mirror that repeats the colors above. · The sun sits low on the right, a small white disk at the horizon. · A warship that fought at Trafalgar in 1805 is shown under tow after being sold to a shipbreaker. The artist probably did not witness the tow and relied on reports."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/slave-ship-slavers-throwing-overboard-the-dead-and-dying-typhoon-coming-on.html","Romanticism","Slave Ship (Slavers Throwing Overboard the Dead and Dying, Typhoon Coming On)","J.M.W. Turner","turner-slave-ship","J.M.W. Turner · 1840 · Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 48 1/4 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Paint is visibly dragged and layered across the whole surface, most of all in the white of the sky. · A vertical band of white and yellow light splits the picture just right of center. · The ship is a few thin dark lines for masts, half hidden by spray, left of center. · Color does the work of drawing: sea, sky and ship dissolve into strokes of red, orange, yellow and white. · The sea is brown, grey and gold, in heavy swirling strokes. · Small forms in the foreground water, a leg, hands, chains, fish and gulls, are the sharpest detail. · The title names the figures in the sea: people thrown overboard."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/rain-steam-and-speed-the-great-western-railway.html","Romanticism","Rain, Steam and Speed – The Great Western Railway","J.M.W. Turner","turner-rain-steam-speed","J.M.W. Turner · 1844 · Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 48 in. The National Gallery, London · Almost everything is blurred into pale haze, with few hard edges. · The engine is the darkest and sharpest shape, a black funnel above a red-orange glow. · A dark bridge drives toward the viewer on a diagonal, its edges spreading apart toward the lower right corner. · Sky, rain and land share the same creams, golds and pale blues, with no clear horizon. · The sky is a field of loose, broken strokes. · A second bridge with arches sits small and pale at the far left. · A train crosses the Maidenhead railway bridge over the Thames; the bridge was finished in 1838."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/heart-of-the-andes.html","Romanticism","Heart of the Andes","Frederic Edwin Church","church-heart-of-the-andes","Frederic Edwin Church · 1859 · Oil on canvas, 66 1/4 × 120 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Frederic Edwin Church (American, 1826–1900). A vast view is painted in sharp, tiny detail from the nearest leaf to the farthest peak. · Single leaves, ferns and tree trunks are drawn one by one in the foreground at right. · Space steps back in clear layers: dark foreground, sunlit plain, brown mountains, a white snow peak. · Light falls in patches, brightest on the waterfall and the pool below. · The snow peak is pale blue-white against blue sky, set at the upper left. · A composite view built from sketches made in Ecuador, first shown in a darkened room under gaslight."],[13,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-rocky-mountains-landers-peak.html","Romanticism","The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak","Albert Bierstadt","bierstadt-rocky-mountains","Albert Bierstadt · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 73 1/2 × 120 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Albert Bierstadt (American, 1830–1902). The far mountains are pale and hazy, almost white, while the near ground is dark green and sharply drawn. · A patch of sunlight in the middle distance lights a waterfall and a cliff, the warmest area. · The tallest peak stands just right of center, soft-edged against the sky. · Figures, horses and tents are small and detailed across the meadow. · The lake is a thin strip, bright where the waterfall is mirrored and dark under the cliff. · Color cools with distance: green, then gold, then blue-white. · The mountains are the Wind River Range, in what is now Wyoming. The picture was made in New York after an 1859 survey trip, and the camp in the foreground is Shoshone."],[14,"/study/value-and-light.html","Value and Light","Value and Light","","cross-fig21","Leonardo da Vinci saw light and shade as what lets a flat picture look solid. In the 1800s, drawing manuals by Mary Merrifield and Anson Cross taught the same ideas with plain white models."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-value-scale.html","Value and Light","The Value Scale","","merrifield-pl02-scale","Value means how light or dark something is, whatever its color, and a value scale lays it out in steps from white to black. · Ten bands step from paper white at the left to solid black at the right, each a shade darker than the last. · Merrifield pointed out that each flat band looks darker along the edge where it meets a lighter one. · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · Odalisque in Grisaille · about 1824–34 · Ingres modeled this unfinished, smaller repeat of his 1814 Grande Odalisque almost entirely in grays, so value does nearly all the work of building the body. · Merrifield noticed that on a lit object most of the surface sits in the middle values, with only small patches at either extreme."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-value-scale.html","Value and Light · Example","Odalisque in Grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","thumb/ingres-odalisque-grisaille","about 1824–34 · Ingres modeled this unfinished, smaller repeat of his 1814 Grande Odalisque almost entirely in grays, so value does nearly all the work of building the body. · The Value Scale"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/local-value-and-light.html","Value and Light","Local Value and Light","","cross-fig07","Every surface has its own lightness, its local value; the light falling on it then adds lights and shadows on top. · The two balls share one light, and each turns from a lit side to a shadow side in the same places. · On the darker ball the whole pattern is shifted down and squeezed into a narrower range, so its shine stands out more. · Francisco de Zurbarán · Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose · 1633 · Light from the left strikes pale lemons, darker oranges and a white cup, and each holds on to its own lightness whether lit or shaded. · Cross noted that the lit side of a dark object can look lighter than the shaded side of a light one."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/local-value-and-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose","Francisco de Zurbarán","thumb/zurbaran-still-life-lemons","1633 · Light from the left strikes pale lemons, darker oranges and a white cup, and each holds on to its own lightness whether lit or shaded. · Local Value and Light"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/high-and-low-key.html","Value and Light","High and Low Key","","morisot-summers-day","Key describes where a picture's values mostly fall: a picture built mainly from lights is high key, one built mainly from darks is low key. · Almost all the values sit at the light end, so the whole scene reads as bright air over water. · The deep blue coat on the left-hand woman is the biggest dark area anywhere in it. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Philosopher in Meditation · 1632 · Darkness fills most of the panel; daylight from the window reaches the old man, and a fire at the right adds one small, separate glow. · Key and contrast are separate: a bright picture may still hold some deep darks, and a dark one some brilliant lights."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/high-and-low-key.html","Value and Light · Example","Philosopher in Meditation","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-philosopher-in-meditation","1632 · Darkness fills most of the panel; daylight from the window reaches the old man, and a fire at the right adds one small, separate glow. · High and Low Key"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Value and Light","The Light and Shadow Sequence","","merrifield-pl09","When one light falls on a rounded form, the values run in a set order: a highlight, the light side, a halftone, the darkest band of shadow, reflected light, then the shadow thrown on the ground. · The lower-right sphere is lit from the upper left: a small shine, then darker tones as the surface turns away, and a shadow on the table. · In the upper-right diagram a line runs from the highlight through the center; Merrifield noted that it crosses the middle of the darkest band. · Juan Sánchez Cotán · Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber · about 1602 · In the dark opening, the lit fronts of the quince and the cabbage give way to deep shadow on the sides farthest from the light. · The darkest band sits a little in from the edge, and light bounced back from nearby surfaces lightens the shadow near the rim."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Value and Light · Example","Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber","Juan Sánchez Cotán","thumb/sanchez-cotan-quince-cabbage","about 1602 · In the dark opening, the lit fronts of the quince and the cabbage give way to deep shadow on the sides farthest from the light. · The Light and Shadow Sequence"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/highlights.html","Value and Light","Highlights","","cross-fig16","On a polished surface, a highlight is a small reflected image of the light source. · The dark bottle shows two small highlights, one for each half of the divided window it faced. · The pale top of the block shows in the bottle's glaze as a curved reflected shape. · Willem Claesz Heda · Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware · 1635 · Tiny, crisp highlights tell us the tall glass and the tipped silver cup are polished; the oysters' wet shine is broader and softer. · The Met points to a gleaming spoon that leads the eye on to a shard of glass and other curving forms."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/highlights.html","Value and Light · Example","Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware","Willem Claesz Heda","thumb/heda-still-life-oysters","1635 · Tiny, crisp highlights tell us the tall glass and the tipped silver cup are polished; the oysters' wet shine is broader and softer. · Highlights"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/cast-shadows.html","Value and Light","Cast Shadows","","merrifield-pl03","When an object stands in the light's path, it throws its shape onto nearby surfaces as a cast shadow. · Each cube throws a band of shadow across the table on the side away from the light. · Each cast shadow is darker than the shaded side of the cube that throws it. · Vilhelm Hammershøi · Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams · 1901 · The window bars block parts of the sunbeams, so the patches of sun on the floor carry the window's grid of shadow. · Cast shadows are usually darkest and crispest next to the object, and which way they point and how far they stretch reveal where the light sits."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/cast-shadows.html","Value and Light · Example","Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","Vilhelm Hammershøi","thumb/hammershoi-dust-motes","1901 · The window bars block parts of the sunbeams, so the patches of sun on the floor carry the window's grid of shadow. · Cast Shadows"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/direction-of-light.html","Value and Light","Direction of Light","","cross-figs-08-10-11","The direction of the light sets how a subject divides between lit and shaded areas. · Top: light from the side splits each white form into a lit half and a shadow half, so the shapes read clearly. · Middle: light from behind the viewer reaches almost every face, and the forms flatten out. · Bottom: light from behind the group leaves most surfaces in shadow, with bright tops and outlines. · Pierre-Paul Prud'hon · Study of Drapery · about 1813 · Light from one side catches the ridges of the cloth in white chalk, the deepest folds go to black, and the blue paper serves as the middle value. · Cross wrote that with the light behind the viewer, what contrast remains comes mostly from local color."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/direction-of-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Study of Drapery","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon","thumb/prudhon-blue-paper","about 1813 · Light from one side catches the ridges of the cloth in white chalk, the deepest folds go to black, and the blue paper serves as the middle value. · Direction of Light"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/hard-and-soft-light.html","Value and Light","Hard and Soft Light","","cross-figs-09-12","The smaller a light looks from where the subject stands, the harder it is: the distant sun gives hard light, a cloudy sky or a big nearby window soft light. · Top: in direct sun the shadows have crisp edges. · Bottom: weak light arriving from several sides wraps around the forms, and the faint shadows fade at their edges. · Joaquín Sorolla · Sewing the Sail · 1896 · Sunshine broken up by the leaves spatters the white sail with crisp spots of light and shade. · Outdoors, Cross observed, light bouncing in from every side makes shadows lighter than they are indoors."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/hard-and-soft-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Sewing the Sail","Joaquín Sorolla","thumb/sorolla-sewing-the-sail","1896 · Sunshine broken up by the leaves spatters the white sail with crisp spots of light and shade. · Hard and Soft Light"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/candlelight.html","Value and Light","Candlelight","","wright-air-pump-detail","A candle or lamp within the scene lights nearby things strongly, and its light drops off fast with distance. · The faces nearest the glass bowl are brightest; those set farther back sink toward shadow. · The flame itself is hidden behind the glass bowl at the bottom, yet where it sits is clear from what it lights. · Georges de La Tour · The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame · about 1635–37 · Her face, hand and sleeve, closest to the flame, are brightest; the skull in her lap is dimmer and her legs fade into shadow. · If the flame itself is shown, it turns into part of the subject and gives the whole picture one bright focus."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/candlelight.html","Value and Light · Example","The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame","Georges de La Tour","thumb/latour-magdalen-smoking-flame","about 1635–37 · Her face, hand and sleeve, closest to the flame, are brightest; the skull in her lap is dimmer and her legs fade into shadow. · Candlelight"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/edges.html","Value and Light","Edges","","cross-fig13","An edge is where one value meets another: hard when the change is sharp, soft when it blends, lost when the two values match. · Inside the block's dark shadow, the cylinder's outline disappears for a short stretch. · Where the white forms meet the darker backdrop, the edges are hard and clear. · Diego Velázquez · Juan de Pareja · 1650 · The white collar has crisp edges, while the beard melts into the hair and the dark coat into the dark background. · Cross taught that where an outline can't be seen, it shouldn't be drawn."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/edges.html","Value and Light · Example","Juan de Pareja","Diego Velázquez","thumb/velazquez-juan-de-pareja","1650 · The white collar has crisp edges, while the beard melts into the hair and the dark coat into the dark background. · Edges"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/chiaroscuro.html","Value and Light","Chiaroscuro","","rembrandt-aristotle-detail","Chiaroscuro (Italian for \"light-dark\") is the balance and pattern of light and shade across a whole picture, arranged as one design. · Only a few things catch the light: the face, the pale sleeve, the hand and the bust it rests on, while everything else drops into brown gloom. · A band of shadow runs over the upper face and keeps the eyes partly veiled. · Leonardo da Vinci · The Virgin of the Rocks · about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · The figures come out of the dark grotto by soft degrees, with no hard outline at the Virgin's temples or nose, an effect later called sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\". · Leonardo built these figures over an underpainting in black and white, so their values were set before color went on."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/chiaroscuro.html","Value and Light · Example","The Virgin of the Rocks","Leonardo da Vinci","thumb/leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks","about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · The figures come out of the dark grotto by soft degrees, with no hard outline at the Virgin's temples or nose, an effect later called sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\". · Chiaroscuro"],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/tenebrism.html","Value and Light","Tenebrism","","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew-detail","Tenebrism, from the Latin tenebrae, \"darkness\", is chiaroscuro at its most extreme: brightly lit figures set against near-total darkness, with little in between. · A single beam enters high at the right, from beyond the frame, and slants down across the seated men. · The painted window above lets in only a dim, diffused glow; the beam is what picks out the figures. · Artemisia Gentileschi · Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes · about 1623–25 · A lone candle lights the room; Judith lifts a hand near it as though shading it, and the maid kneeling below catches the same light. · The Detroit Institute of Arts links this painting's theatrical lighting to Caravaggio."],[14,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/tenebrism.html","Value and Light · Example","Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes","Artemisia Gentileschi","thumb/gentileschi-judith-detroit","about 1623–25 · A lone candle lights the room; Judith lifts a hand near it as though shading it, and the maid kneeling below catches the same light. · Tenebrism"]]};
